- Sargodha.
- Yes.

688
00:52:45,104 --> 00:52:46,854
Sargodha. And

689
00:52:48,271 --> 00:52:49,646
the robbers are here.

690
00:52:50,687 --> 00:52:52,146
The roads are closed.

691
00:52:52,687 --> 00:52:55,396
A border was created,
after the Partition.

692
00:52:57,271 --> 00:53:03,396
The Great Wall of China
can be seen from the moon.

693
00:53:04,646 --> 00:53:05,937
I've seen it.

694
00:53:06,312 --> 00:53:07,979
Is he better? Or worse?

695
00:53:08,062 --> 00:53:11,562
He's the same. Dementia has set in.

696
00:53:11,729 --> 00:53:13,521
Let's go. It's only 100 miles away.

697
00:53:13,812 --> 00:53:16,146
Sargodha is 400 kilometers away.

698
00:53:16,479 --> 00:53:18,646
No, it's close by.

699
00:53:20,229 --> 00:53:24,104
Take the dirt roads, through the fields.

700
00:53:32,646 --> 00:53:33,562
Jiya.

701
00:53:35,271 --> 00:53:36,854
I don't know what came over me.

702
00:53:38,729 --> 00:53:40,187
I'll never do it again.

703
00:53:41,062 --> 00:53:42,104
I promise you.

704
00:53:43,187 --> 00:53:44,854
And what if we got married?

705
00:53:49,312 --> 00:53:50,562
You still won't do it?

706
00:53:53,937 --> 00:53:57,187
Take your promise back! Hurry!

707
00:53:57,271 --> 00:53:58,229
I take it back.

708
00:54:39,479 --> 00:54:41,896
No one will believe that you're in town.

709
00:54:44,812 --> 00:54:47,312
When did you arrive in Chandigarh?

710
00:54:47,604 --> 00:54:48,562
Last week.

711
00:54:49,312 --> 00:54:51,562
It wasn't planned. So how come?

712
00:54:51,812 --> 00:54:52,771
Just like that.

713
00:54:53,479 --> 00:54:56,271
"Just like that?" I've heard that before.

714
00:54:56,771 --> 00:54:59,521
Today Nirvair will tell us what it means.

715
00:54:59,646 --> 00:55:00,562
- Right?
- But...

716
00:55:00,646 --> 00:55:03,687
You must enlighten us. No, please!

717
00:55:04,979 --> 00:55:07,562
My grandpa is on his deathbed.

718
00:55:08,604 --> 00:55:09,479
What?

719
00:55:10,021 --> 00:55:12,146
He's dying, so I came to see him.

720
00:55:13,937 --> 00:55:17,271
Everyone has to go one day,
so why spoil your mood?

721
00:55:18,062 --> 00:55:19,604
It's not that serious.

722
00:55:21,104 --> 00:55:22,396
In fact it's quite funny.

723
00:55:23,104 --> 00:55:24,646
- Let me--
- No, honestly.

724
00:55:24,729 --> 00:55:29,104
He's 95 years old and he thinks
he's still in college.

725
00:55:30,646 --> 00:55:32,729
To him, it's 1947.

726
00:55:33,104 --> 00:55:35,437
He keeps asking to go to Sargodha.

727
00:55:35,771 --> 00:55:38,187
- And you know, Sargodha is...
- Across the border.

728
00:55:38,396 --> 00:55:40,312
Does your grandpa want to go
there too?

729
00:55:40,396 --> 00:55:42,771
- No, to Rawalpindi.
- To Lahore.

730
00:55:42,896 --> 00:55:44,937
- To Peshawar.
- Gujranwala.

731
00:55:45,021 --> 00:55:45,896
Oh!

732
00:55:46,396 --> 00:55:47,812
Many refugees here!

733
00:55:48,812 --> 00:55:50,979
You must have heard the word
''Partition?''

734
00:55:51,729 --> 00:55:54,687
Partition caused great devastation.
Millions died.

735
00:55:55,021 --> 00:55:57,771
With little warning, people had to abandon
their homes.

736
00:55:58,021 --> 00:55:59,729
Know the problem with partition?

737
00:56:00,896 --> 00:56:02,062
There was no internet.

738
00:56:03,646 --> 00:56:07,062
Otherwise I would've figured out
what was going on in grandpa's mind.

739
00:56:07,312 --> 00:56:10,062
If social media existed, we would have
seen his updates.

740
00:56:10,687 --> 00:56:13,687
Your grandpa would be tagged.
Your grandma too.

741
00:56:15,062 --> 00:56:16,354
"Leaving Lahore."

742
00:56:16,437 --> 00:56:18,687
"Watch my live stream
from the train roof!"

743
00:56:18,771 --> 00:56:21,437
<i>Come, let us go under love's shade...</i>

744
00:56:22,062 --> 00:56:26,354
"If you survive, like, comment
and subscribe. Hashtag, Partition."

745
00:56:27,021 --> 00:56:28,229
They had no mobiles.

746
00:56:29,312 --> 00:56:32,229
How did those people get from there
to here without phones?

747
00:56:32,521 --> 00:56:34,479
If it were me, I would open a map.

748
00:56:37,021 --> 00:56:40,687
''How long will walking
from Lahore to Delhi take?''

749
00:56:41,687 --> 00:56:43,312
"Fifteen days, Seventeen hours."

750
00:56:45,021 --> 00:56:47,979
Fifteen days by foot? Forget it.
Let's stay.

751
00:56:48,146 --> 00:56:49,687
Dictatorship is fine!

752
00:56:51,312 --> 00:56:54,604
Even when there's nothing happening,
social media can cause havoc.

753
00:56:54,854 --> 00:56:56,812
And here was the division of a country.

754
00:56:57,146 --> 00:56:58,521
Look at the content!

755
00:56:58,979 --> 00:57:00,562
Two million people were killed.

756
00:57:01,437 --> 00:57:04,312
Imagine the number of likes and comments
we would've seen.

757
00:57:05,354 --> 00:57:07,521
The English missed out on the action.

758
00:57:08,104 --> 00:57:09,687
Hashtag, divide and rule.

759
00:57:09,771 --> 00:57:11,187
Hashtag, missing the spice.

760
00:57:12,979 --> 00:57:15,479
Sirs, the hatred that you have spread

761
00:57:15,729 --> 00:57:17,646
we enjoy for 78 years.

762
00:57:17,812 --> 00:57:19,562
Please enjoy our Kohinoor.

763
00:57:21,021 --> 00:57:24,354
For two hundred years you messed up
our motherland

764
00:57:24,687 --> 00:57:28,062
now we'll mess up your mother tongue
forever!

765
00:57:42,146 --> 00:57:43,604
These are from Sargodha.

766
00:57:44,312 --> 00:57:47,687
I hear they had high quality cameras
in Punjab before the Partition.

767
00:57:51,187 --> 00:57:54,854
I think I've seen these photos before.
Or ones like them.

768
00:57:55,437 --> 00:57:57,979
"Back to Roots" is an agency in Lahore.

769
00:57:58,437 --> 00:57:59,812
They contributed.

770
00:58:00,104 --> 00:58:03,646
And it had a terrifying effect on people,
like this wall...

771
00:58:04,521 --> 00:58:06,729
- Is that how the border was created?
- Yes.

772
00:58:09,437 --> 00:58:10,646
Do people know all this?

773
00:58:12,271 --> 00:58:15,229
{\an8}Does anyone know
what the India-Pakistan border is called?

774
00:58:15,312 --> 00:58:17,854
{\an8}- The Radcliffe Line.
- Correct! The Radcliffe Line.

775
00:58:18,312 --> 00:58:20,812
{\an8}Sir Radcliffe came from England
to draw the line.

776
00:58:22,937 --> 00:58:27,229
{\an8}"Sir Radcliffe, you must be familiar
with every inch of India?''

777
00:58:27,312 --> 00:58:31,021
{\an8}"No, no, I do not know India well."

778
00:58:31,521 --> 00:58:32,812
{\an8}"This is my first trip!"

779
00:58:34,854 --> 00:58:37,937
{\an8}"But sir, you're demarcating
the India-Pakistan border?"

780
00:58:38,437 --> 00:58:41,312
{\an8}"You're deciding which area goes
to which country."

781
00:58:42,229 --> 00:58:45,437
{\an8}"Me? I know nothing at all
about this place."

782
00:58:45,854 --> 00:58:48,396
"That's why I'll be neutral.
I will not take sides."

783
00:58:49,271 --> 00:58:53,312
{\an8}"By that logic, a tailor can perform
heart surgery!"

784
00:58:54,729 --> 00:58:55,979
{\an8}"He may not know much

785
00:58:56,937 --> 00:58:59,646
{\an8}but he knows how to cut and stitch
on the dotted line."

786
00:59:00,062 --> 00:59:03,062
{\an8}"Anyhow, Mr. Radcliffe,
if you have to do it

787
00:59:03,146 --> 00:59:05,229
{\an8}then take your time."

788
00:59:05,312 --> 00:59:07,396
{\an8}"Oh yes, yes, I'll take my time."

789
00:59:07,729 --> 00:59:09,812
{\an8}"For this task, Viceroy

790
00:59:09,896 --> 00:59:12,896
{\an8}Lord Mountbatten has given me
five weeks."

791
00:59:13,396 --> 00:59:14,937
"Five weeks?"

792
00:59:15,437 --> 00:59:17,729
"You won't even be able
to survey the borders."

793
00:59:17,812 --> 00:59:18,854
"Correct!"

794
00:59:18,979 --> 00:59:24,021
"Mr. Gandhi said, 'See no evil,'
so I won't look."

795
00:59:24,312 --> 00:59:27,062
"I will not set foot outside
the Delhi guesthouse."

796
00:59:27,437 --> 00:59:29,646
"I'll sit at the table
and draw the border

797
00:59:29,729 --> 00:59:32,187
then straight to the airport,
take off to London!"

798
00:59:32,521 --> 00:59:34,312
{\an8}"Or else you lot will kill me!"

799
00:59:41,937 --> 00:59:44,062
The Martians

800
00:59:45,396 --> 00:59:48,021
want to seize the moon.

801
00:59:49,896 --> 00:59:55,021
One by one they gathered,
never walking solo

802
00:59:55,812 --> 00:59:58,479
always in a group.

803
00:59:59,062 --> 01:00:01,854
You can't come here anymore.
Go away.

804
01:00:03,687 --> 01:00:04,812
Why? What happened?

805
01:00:05,354 --> 01:00:07,229
They sent me a petition.

806
01:00:08,896 --> 01:00:12,729
"Kindly relocate to another planet."

807
01:00:13,646 --> 01:00:15,937
What are you staring at?
Get lost!

808
01:00:24,021 --> 01:00:26,396
I'm not going. Do what you want.

809
01:00:31,021 --> 01:00:32,437
Did I ask for the Partition?

810
01:00:35,021 --> 01:00:39,562
How do you know if this spot is in India
or in the new country?

811
01:00:40,437 --> 01:00:41,396
How do we know?

812
01:00:42,437 --> 01:00:45,979
Who cares! This is my home.
I'm not moving.

813
01:00:50,854 --> 01:00:52,104
Who is this rude fellow?

814
01:00:53,146 --> 01:00:56,646
- We must teach him a lesson.
- Yes, set an example with him!

815
01:00:57,229 --> 01:01:00,521
Afzal said to make an example
of these people.

816
01:01:00,771 --> 01:01:01,646
Hey.

817
01:01:02,271 --> 01:01:05,021
- How dare you!
- We'll show you which country you're in.

818
01:01:05,104 --> 01:01:06,854
- Let go of me!
- Let him go.

819
01:01:07,812 --> 01:01:08,646
Get up.

820
01:01:11,396 --> 01:01:12,271
Move back.

821
01:01:15,562 --> 01:01:16,479
Stand up!

822
01:01:16,729 --> 01:01:19,521
Pandit Nehru and even Mr. Jinnah have said

823
01:01:20,687 --> 01:01:24,437
that no one will be forced to leave home
and move to the other country.

824
01:01:24,521 --> 01:01:27,646
Alright. Get up.

825
01:01:28,271 --> 01:01:29,646
We're free now.

826
01:01:30,604 --> 01:01:31,812
Didn't we want freedom?

827
01:01:33,187 --> 01:01:34,937
So now we're fighting each other?

828
01:01:36,896 --> 01:01:37,979
It's sad.

829
01:01:40,812 --> 01:01:42,229
The enemy is leaving

830
01:01:43,229 --> 01:01:45,312
and now we're turning against each other.

831
01:01:47,604 --> 01:01:48,729
But that's the reality.

832
01:01:50,729 --> 01:01:52,229
Far worse will follow.

833
01:01:53,812 --> 01:01:57,229
When it happens, neither Nehru nor Jinnah
will be around to save you.

834
01:01:59,146 --> 01:02:01,771
Troops are being brought in from outside
to Sargodha.

835
01:02:04,229 --> 01:02:05,937
Baloch, Pathan, the tribes.

836
01:02:06,771 --> 01:02:08,104
They won't listen to me.

837
01:02:11,146 --> 01:02:12,396
So don't come here again.

838
01:02:14,437 --> 01:02:16,146
You'll get into trouble here.

839
01:02:17,104 --> 01:02:18,062
Go.

840
01:02:19,521 --> 01:02:21,937
Heard of Adolf Hitler?

841
01:02:25,021 --> 01:02:28,771
He was from Mars too. A Martian.

842
01:02:30,521 --> 01:02:31,396
Yes.

843
01:02:32,896 --> 01:02:35,062
He came to Lahore to become
an actor.

844
01:02:35,437 --> 01:02:40,396
No one cast him. So you know what he did?

845
01:02:42,271 --> 01:02:43,604
He started World War II?

846
01:02:45,937 --> 01:02:47,479
No, he started crying.

847
01:02:48,729 --> 01:02:54,521
For 520/2 years

848
01:02:54,937 --> 01:02:56,021
he kept crying.

849
01:02:56,604 --> 01:02:57,687
He kept crying.

850
01:02:58,021 --> 01:03:00,396
- 520/2?
- 20/2...

851
01:03:00,687 --> 01:03:03,729
- So for 260 years?
- No.

852
01:03:05,812 --> 01:03:10,896
- 520/2.
- Okay.

853
01:03:11,812 --> 01:03:17,979
So I said okay, and got him a role.

854
01:03:18,562 --> 01:03:20,646
For Hitler? Very good.

855
01:03:21,521 --> 01:03:23,854
- Did the movie get made, Grandpa?
- Yes.

856
01:03:24,229 --> 01:03:26,312
- In Sargodha?
- Berlin.

857
01:03:26,979 --> 01:03:29,104
Chest out! Fierce eyes!

858
01:03:29,771 --> 01:03:31,062
Look at the camera.

859
01:03:31,187 --> 01:03:33,271
Blitzkrieg.

860
01:03:34,396 --> 01:03:38,187
Directed by Inderjeet Grewal.

861
01:03:38,312 --> 01:03:39,646
Sir, what are you doing?

862
01:03:39,729 --> 01:03:41,437
Keenu, hold the camera.

863
01:03:43,687 --> 01:03:45,354
Who's Inderjeet Grewal, Grandpa?

864
01:03:46,271 --> 01:03:47,187
Who is he?

865
01:03:48,521 --> 01:03:49,729
The director.

866
01:03:52,271 --> 01:03:54,812
"World War II" is his film

867
01:03:55,521 --> 01:03:57,854
starring Adolf Hitler.

868
01:03:58,187 --> 01:04:01,729
Come here. Hold it. Stay still, okay?

869
01:04:02,729 --> 01:04:04,812
Ikonta 520/2, made in Berlin.

870
01:04:05,562 --> 01:04:09,437
All photos of the World War were taken
with it. Got it from the war front!

871
01:04:12,396 --> 01:04:14,104
Look fierce! At the camera.

872
01:04:14,646 --> 01:04:16,937
Keenu, press the button.

873
01:04:17,729 --> 01:04:19,812
Three, two, one and...

874
01:04:25,562 --> 01:04:26,812
I'm sorry to say this

875
01:04:26,896 --> 01:04:30,979
but Sikhs and Hindus here
outnumber Muslims three to one.

876
01:04:32,354 --> 01:04:33,729
So why do we need to fear?

877
01:04:35,437 --> 01:04:38,812
The English population
in India was small.

878
01:04:39,812 --> 01:04:41,937
Yet they managed to rule us
for 200 years.

879
01:04:42,354 --> 01:04:45,021
They deceived us, by illegal means.

880
01:04:45,271 --> 01:04:46,146
Yes.

881
01:04:46,521 --> 01:04:51,229
Taking lessons from them, the traitors
are calling tribes here to attack us.

882
01:04:51,479 --> 01:04:53,521
This isn't the first time.

883
01:04:54,521 --> 01:04:59,104
Since the time of Alexander the Great,
people have attacked us.

884
01:05:00,437 --> 01:05:01,812
But we are Punjab.

885
01:05:02,437 --> 01:05:03,646
We cannot be broken.

886
01:05:14,646 --> 01:05:17,229
What's wrong? Seen her?

887
01:05:19,687 --> 01:05:20,854
Is everything okay?

888
01:05:22,271 --> 01:05:23,354
Oh, Phoola!

889
01:05:25,187 --> 01:05:28,146
There's fire burning on both sides.

890
01:05:28,604 --> 01:05:29,562
What?

891
01:05:31,729 --> 01:05:32,562
I mean...

892
01:05:34,812 --> 01:05:36,729
she has said yes.

893
01:05:38,396 --> 01:05:39,812
Isn't that good?

894
01:05:42,896 --> 01:05:43,812
We have...

895
01:05:46,354 --> 01:05:48,437
decided to get married.

896
01:05:49,312 --> 01:05:52,521
So why do you look like
you're about to die?

897
01:05:52,812 --> 01:05:54,187
This is wonderful news!

898
01:05:54,312 --> 01:05:55,812
This is how love feels.

899
01:05:56,562 --> 01:05:58,062
In the poet's words...

900
01:05:59,937 --> 01:06:02,104
<i>The river of love flows backwards</i>

901
01:06:02,937 --> 01:06:05,646
<i>The one who dives in, drowns</i>

902
01:06:06,854 --> 01:06:09,062
<i>The one who drowns, gets across</i>

903
01:06:09,187 --> 01:06:11,729
Oh my! I want to drown too!

904
01:06:11,896 --> 01:06:13,479
Find someone for me too, Keenu.

905
01:06:14,937 --> 01:06:16,729
First let me get my problems sorted.

906
01:06:17,021 --> 01:06:19,104
You always think about yourself!

907
01:06:19,896 --> 01:06:20,854
Okay, go on.

908
01:06:21,729 --> 01:06:23,146
What can I say, Phoola?

909
01:06:27,437 --> 01:06:28,396
I'm lost for words.

910
01:06:32,187 --> 01:06:38,312
Besides, Phoola, I can't tell you
what happened.

911
01:06:38,729 --> 01:06:40,104
Oh, my!

912
01:06:42,437 --> 01:06:43,687
- What is it, Pali?
- Hey!

913
01:06:44,979 --> 01:06:46,021
Go!

914
01:06:53,229 --> 01:06:56,521
The college is closed.
So what are you studying?

915
01:06:58,229 --> 01:06:59,396
Poetry.

916
01:07:00,187 --> 01:07:01,146
Go to sleep.

917
01:07:02,521 --> 01:07:03,437
Let me hear it.

918
01:07:13,312 --> 01:07:15,146
<i>The season is about to change</i>

919
01:07:15,396 --> 01:07:16,312
Okay.

920
01:07:18,729 --> 01:07:21,146
<i>A bud is about to blossom</i>

921
01:07:21,312 --> 01:07:22,437
Okay.

922
01:07:22,937 --> 01:07:23,896
Don't say "okay."

923
01:07:28,312 --> 01:07:30,812
<i>Western rule will end</i>

924
01:07:32,854 --> 01:07:35,146
<i>The dawn of freedom shall break</i>

925
01:07:37,771 --> 01:07:40,271
<i>Nothing will keep us apart</i>

926
01:07:41,937 --> 01:07:46,896
<i>You and me, we'll be forever one</i>

927
01:07:52,312 --> 01:07:54,396
Stick to cricket, Brother!

928
01:07:56,604 --> 01:08:00,562
Eyes shut! You've got a minute
to fall asleep.

929
01:08:10,146 --> 01:08:11,354
Inderjeet Grewal?

930
01:08:11,521 --> 01:08:12,437
That's right.

931
01:08:13,729 --> 01:08:16,312
I think I heard the name
when I was a child.

932
01:08:17,021 --> 01:08:19,104
Uncle, remember anything?

933
01:08:19,646 --> 01:08:20,562
Why?

934
01:08:20,979 --> 01:08:24,771
Grandpa mentioned him
when he was talking to Nivi.

935
01:08:26,021 --> 01:08:29,396
He only talks to Nivi.
No one else.

936
01:08:30,187 --> 01:08:33,229
Grandpa Pali, he's very troubled.

937
01:08:34,312 --> 01:08:35,937
But he isn't letting go.

938
01:08:36,729 --> 01:08:39,229
I think something about the past
is tormenting him.

939
01:08:39,521 --> 01:08:41,979
So what can you do about it?

940
01:08:42,937 --> 01:08:45,937
We can try and find out
what he's looking for.

941
01:08:46,521 --> 01:08:47,562
What?

942
01:08:47,896 --> 01:08:48,812
Closure.

943
01:08:49,437 --> 01:08:53,562
I'm trying to understand
what he wants.

944
01:08:54,229 --> 01:08:57,354
He talks, but I can't always
understand what he's saying.

945
01:08:57,521 --> 01:09:01,937
How could you understand?
Were you there at that time?

946
01:09:02,312 --> 01:09:03,937
Were you there?

947
01:09:05,729 --> 01:09:07,437
It's his ordeal.

948
01:09:07,646 --> 01:09:09,979
It'll turn to ash with him
on his funeral pyre.

949
01:09:10,437 --> 01:09:13,854
It's his destiny. Don't upset him.

950
01:09:14,854 --> 01:09:17,312
Never mind, Uncle.

951
01:09:22,229 --> 01:09:23,979
Circumstances have made us hurry.

952
01:09:24,312 --> 01:09:27,354
Engagements don't usually take place
in the month of Ramadan.

953
01:09:30,521 --> 01:09:32,521
What is it?

954
01:09:37,354 --> 01:09:38,312
Phoola!

955
01:09:38,896 --> 01:09:41,896
Aftab has come.
Greet him.

956
01:09:43,021 --> 01:09:46,271
Hello, Aftab. How are you?

957
01:09:47,854 --> 01:09:50,312
- How's Rumana?
- That's why I'm here.

958
01:09:50,854 --> 01:09:53,479
Father says you must not come
to Rumana's engagement.

959
01:09:54,146 --> 01:09:57,437
It's on August 14.
That's Pakistan's Independence Day.

960
01:09:57,521 --> 01:09:58,687
There could be trouble.

961
01:09:59,479 --> 01:10:00,896
Tell me something.

962
01:10:01,271 --> 01:10:05,021
Does a map exist of the two new countries
being created?

963
01:10:05,604 --> 01:10:06,812
Where's the border?

964
01:10:07,604 --> 01:10:10,979
- If you want to cross it, how to find it?
- You're right.

965
01:10:11,312 --> 01:10:12,729
Name me one city

966
01:10:12,812 --> 01:10:15,854
that is not inhabited by Hindus,
Sikhs and Muslims.

967
01:10:17,229 --> 01:10:19,146
Is there even one city?

968
01:10:19,729 --> 01:10:23,396
Aftab, do you think people belonging
to every community

969
01:10:23,479 --> 01:10:27,187
will abandon their homes in the city
and village to move to the other side?

970
01:10:27,396 --> 01:10:28,896
Is that likely?

971
01:10:29,521 --> 01:10:31,604
Bravo, Keenu, bravo!

972
01:10:32,021 --> 01:10:36,646
I bow down to your spirit
of communal harmony!

973
01:10:41,687 --> 01:10:45,354
Think of the people whose homes
were split apart by Radcliffe's Line.

974
01:10:45,896 --> 01:10:48,062
A guy wakes up in the morning.
Oh, God!

975
01:10:48,146 --> 01:10:50,979
The kitchen is in Pakistan
and the dining table in India.

976
01:10:51,396 --> 01:10:53,771
So which flag must he fly
to celebrate freedom?

977
01:10:55,479 --> 01:10:57,021
This was the case for many.

978
01:10:57,104 --> 01:10:59,937
Independence was declared
on August 14 and 15.

979
01:11:00,396 --> 01:11:03,729
but the border was announced on August 17.

980
01:11:05,854 --> 01:11:07,979
"Brother Mountbatten, what have you done?"

981
01:11:08,062 --> 01:11:11,146
"You don't get it.
It's for your own good."

982
01:11:11,229 --> 01:11:15,521
"If people knew at Independence
where the border was, there'd be disorder

983
01:11:16,521 --> 01:11:17,729
and rioting."

984
01:11:18,479 --> 01:11:21,687
"When they hear about the border later,
will there be no riots?"

985
01:11:21,771 --> 01:11:24,854
"Sure. There will be riots,
but we would've left by then."

986
01:11:25,437 --> 01:11:27,562
''On August 14th, we'll vanish!''

987
01:11:35,271 --> 01:11:37,354
Pakistan Independence celebration.

988
01:11:38,229 --> 01:11:41,437
They look happy. It doesn't look unsafe.

989
01:11:42,271 --> 01:11:44,354
Don't raise the curtain!

990
01:11:45,812 --> 01:11:48,812
Phoola! Pull it down.
Don't show your face.

991
01:11:49,104 --> 01:11:50,146
Pull it down.

992
01:11:52,521 --> 01:11:56,229
Today our dream has come true!

993
01:11:58,187 --> 01:12:00,562
- Give us some sweets too.
- Today, our country...

994
01:12:00,646 --> 01:12:02,312
Give us some.

995
01:12:02,396 --> 01:12:04,396
Give us some.

996
01:12:04,479 --> 01:12:07,312
All this land, this air,
these surroundings

997
01:12:09,146 --> 01:12:11,229
it all belongs to us now!

998
01:12:15,812 --> 01:12:18,729
<i>Don't be mean</i>

999
01:12:20,687 --> 01:12:25,021
<i>Don't be mean</i>

1000
01:12:25,187 --> 01:12:29,979
<i>Have some shame!
You have fathered children too</i>

1001
01:12:30,146 --> 01:12:34,437
All the girls have dressed up
to be photographed!

1002
01:12:34,521 --> 01:12:36,854
That's smart!

1003
01:12:37,271 --> 01:12:38,771
- Look in this direction.
- Yes.

1004
01:12:38,854 --> 01:12:41,479
Look here. Hey, wait.

1005
01:12:43,354 --> 01:12:46,187
Ready? Three, two, one, and click.

1006
01:13:09,396 --> 01:13:11,604
- Greetings.
- Greetings.

1007
01:13:12,646 --> 01:13:13,979
Why did I send you there?

1008
01:13:14,979 --> 01:13:15,979
Why did I?

1009
01:13:16,146 --> 01:13:17,562
Uncle Inder brought us.

1010
01:13:18,396 --> 01:13:21,854
He's a war veteran.
He fought in the World War.

1011
01:13:22,521 --> 01:13:24,229
That's when he went crazy!

1012
01:13:25,354 --> 01:13:27,521
Will he protect you with his camera?

1013
01:13:27,979 --> 01:13:32,396
Ikonta 520/2, made in Berlin.
Most versatile and suitable for portraits.

1014
01:13:32,479 --> 01:13:34,687
Don't you know the situation?

1015
01:13:35,104 --> 01:13:36,562
And you bring the girls here?

1016
01:13:36,687 --> 01:13:38,021
I invited them, Father.

1017
01:13:38,812 --> 01:13:41,062
I can't get engaged without my friends.

1018
01:13:41,187 --> 01:13:43,271
We want lovely photos.

1019
01:13:43,354 --> 01:13:45,021
Don't talk nonsense!

1020
01:13:45,604 --> 01:13:47,187
Have you all gone crazy?

1021
01:13:47,521 --> 01:13:50,812
How unlucky I am to get scolded
on my engagement day.

1022
01:13:51,562 --> 01:13:55,396
Did I ask the world to go crazy?
How is it my fault?

1023
01:13:55,937 --> 01:13:58,729
I wanted some nice photos
of my engagement.

1024
01:13:59,271 --> 01:14:01,937
Everyone has them. Why not me?

1025
01:14:03,187 --> 01:14:06,104
Dear, must you cry?

1026
01:14:07,104 --> 01:14:08,812
Are you a child to cry like this?

1027
01:14:10,354 --> 01:14:12,937
- You're getting married.
- Father!

1028
01:14:13,062 --> 01:14:14,979
It's unbefitting.

1029
01:14:15,937 --> 01:14:17,312
Stop crying.

1030
01:14:18,104 --> 01:14:19,146
Stop it now.

1031
01:14:20,021 --> 01:14:21,312
A poem.

1032
01:14:22,187 --> 01:14:24,271
A poem sounds best
in the poet's voice.

1033
01:14:26,396 --> 01:14:27,229
Father.

1034
01:14:30,396 --> 01:14:32,479
Afzal is here.

1035
01:14:34,021 --> 01:14:35,146
Close all the windows!

1036
01:14:35,479 --> 01:14:36,854
Don't let them see you.

1037
01:14:45,729 --> 01:14:47,812
- Jalal!
- Yes, sir.

1038
01:14:48,646 --> 01:14:49,937
Who let them in?

1039
01:14:50,854 --> 01:14:51,729
Greetings, sir.

1040
01:14:52,854 --> 01:14:53,729
Come.

1041
01:14:56,896 --> 01:14:57,854
Step back!

1042
01:14:59,437 --> 01:15:01,021
I'm sorry to bother you, sir.

1043
01:15:02,521 --> 01:15:04,146
But your brother lives in Delhi

1044
01:15:05,312 --> 01:15:07,521
and the situation there is terrible.

1045
01:15:08,187 --> 01:15:10,354
Armies of different religions are forming.

1046
01:15:11,521 --> 01:15:13,521
And our people
are in the minority there.

1047
01:15:15,021 --> 01:15:16,062
Anything else?

1048
01:15:19,396 --> 01:15:21,979
Sir, my whole family is in Delhi.

1049
01:15:22,687 --> 01:15:26,062
Many people are getting together
and coming here by train.

1050
01:15:27,812 --> 01:15:29,937
If your brother was among them

1051
01:15:31,312 --> 01:15:32,354
they'd be safe.

1052
01:15:34,104 --> 01:15:36,187
Another thing, sir.

1053
01:15:37,187 --> 01:15:39,312
Before outside forces arrive here

1054
01:15:41,437 --> 01:15:43,521
it would be better

1055
01:15:44,271 --> 01:15:48,062
if you requested your non-Muslims friends
to leave the city.

1056
01:15:51,187 --> 01:15:54,229
For God's sake, do not allow them
in your house

1057
01:15:56,437 --> 01:15:58,521
or who knows

1058
01:15:59,729 --> 01:16:01,354
fingers may be pointed at you.

1059
01:16:02,812 --> 01:16:05,021
If I needed advice

1060
01:16:06,271 --> 01:16:08,354
then, believe me, Afzal

1061
01:16:09,604 --> 01:16:11,812
you would be the last person
I would ask.

1062
01:16:14,937 --> 01:16:16,687
The next time you come here

1063
01:16:18,187 --> 01:16:19,354
get permission first.

1064
01:16:22,312 --> 01:16:23,396
Come on!

1065
01:16:24,979 --> 01:16:25,854
Here.

1066
01:16:28,229 --> 01:16:30,312
You're a lion! Go!

1067
01:16:34,354 --> 01:16:35,437
What are you up to?

1068
01:16:35,604 --> 01:16:36,562
Excuse me?

1069
01:16:36,937 --> 01:16:38,229
When did we last meet?

1070
01:16:41,229 --> 01:16:45,562
When the wind blows at night,
I imagine rioters chasing you.

1071
01:16:45,771 --> 01:16:48,521
I wake up with a start and think
you have left Sargodha

1072
01:16:48,604 --> 01:16:51,104
and I don't even know
if it's my imagination.

1073
01:16:51,396 --> 01:16:53,479
Does it make sense?

1074
01:16:57,229 --> 01:16:59,312
Okay, now I see you.

1075
01:17:00,312 --> 01:17:04,146
But when the wind blows again
at night

1076
01:17:04,646 --> 01:17:06,854
I'll get scared again,
thinking you had left.

1077
01:17:06,937 --> 01:17:08,146
What will I do then?

1078
01:17:12,646 --> 01:17:14,229
You're standing like a statue.

1079
01:17:15,437 --> 01:17:17,437
You really like me, don't you?

1080
01:17:18,312 --> 01:17:21,104
Isn't that the most foolish thing
to say now?

1081
01:17:22,062 --> 01:17:23,146
That's true.

1082
01:17:27,812 --> 01:17:28,812
Jalal!

1083
01:17:29,354 --> 01:17:31,437
Jiya, I'm not leaving.

1084
01:17:32,187 --> 01:17:33,229
I promise.

1085
01:17:39,729 --> 01:17:40,687
Say it.

1086
01:17:41,229 --> 01:17:42,521
Get the carriage.

1087
01:17:42,937 --> 01:17:46,521
Take the Grewals home.
No one must see them.

1088
01:17:46,604 --> 01:17:51,437
You've caused me trouble.
Take it back, I don't want it.

1089
01:17:52,937 --> 01:17:54,229
Jiya, I'm not leaving you.

1090
01:17:54,979 --> 01:17:56,104
No matter what.

1091
01:17:58,312 --> 01:18:02,396
When the rioters show up,
all your bravery will go up in smoke.

1092
01:18:03,521 --> 01:18:05,521
You won't even think of me then.

1093
01:18:07,396 --> 01:18:09,146
- Of course I will.
- But how?

1094
01:18:09,437 --> 01:18:14,312
I'll write your name on my hand,
like I write a poem to memorize it.

1095
01:18:15,271 --> 01:18:18,271
That's the second foolish thing
you've said today.

1096
01:18:18,604 --> 01:18:20,104
My name will get rubbed off.

1097
01:18:24,812 --> 01:18:28,021
I will never leave you, Jiya.
Trust me.

1098
01:18:29,437 --> 01:18:32,146
Why do I have this premonition
that you'll leave?

1099
01:18:33,396 --> 01:18:36,896
Oh, God! My premonitions
have never been wrong.

1100
01:18:37,229 --> 01:18:39,437
- I'm...
- Bring the carriage to the courtyard.

1101
01:18:42,437 --> 01:18:44,604
My poem!

1102
01:18:52,312 --> 01:18:54,396
Every day I worry.

1103
01:18:56,021 --> 01:18:58,812
I don't know if you're here
or gone.

1104
01:19:00,479 --> 01:19:03,021
Not knowing will kill me, Keenu.

1105
01:19:06,312 --> 01:19:08,312
Set my mind to rest.

1106
01:19:08,396 --> 01:19:09,979
I will.

1107
01:19:11,146 --> 01:19:12,146
How?

1108
01:19:19,271 --> 01:19:20,229
Jiya!

1109
01:19:28,229 --> 01:19:32,104
A poem sounds best
in the poet's voice.

1110
01:20:16,479 --> 01:20:18,146
There's a groundwater crisis.

1111
01:20:19,104 --> 01:20:22,146
The water level is decreasing,
but we seem to ignore it

1112
01:20:22,271 --> 01:20:25,229
and we carry on sowing seeds that need
maximum watering.

1113
01:20:25,979 --> 01:20:29,437
As it is, our fields are full
of pesticides and fertilizers.

1114
01:20:30,062 --> 01:20:32,229
The air used to be clean.

1115
01:20:32,437 --> 01:20:36,062
Now we are polluting it
by burning farm stubble.

1116
01:20:38,062 --> 01:20:39,312
We have many problems.

1117
01:20:40,271 --> 01:20:42,604
But don't think there isn't a solution.

1118
01:20:42,854 --> 01:20:44,187
There is one.

1119
01:20:44,854 --> 01:20:47,354
In fact, there is one solution
to all our problems.

1120
01:20:47,479 --> 01:20:48,437
What?

1121
01:20:48,646 --> 01:20:50,687
Get a visa and leave the country.

1122
01:20:51,646 --> 01:20:53,354
- So funny!
- Canada, U.S., U.K.!

1123
01:20:53,479 --> 01:20:55,562
Whoever lets you in, go!

1124
01:20:55,937 --> 01:20:57,896
He wants to be a stand-up comedian too.

1125
01:20:59,312 --> 01:21:00,979
There's a technique to converting

1126
01:21:01,729 --> 01:21:04,104
farm stubble into compost
instead of burning it.

1127
01:21:04,396 --> 01:21:05,271
Really?

1128
01:21:05,562 --> 01:21:07,937
Many countries having started
doing it.

1129
01:21:08,521 --> 01:21:12,521
The farmer gets free fodder
and saves transportation money.

1130
01:21:12,854 --> 01:21:15,229
That's what we need.
Why can't it be done here?

1131
01:21:15,604 --> 01:21:16,604
Of course it can.

1132
01:21:17,312 --> 01:21:19,979
We need to make a prototype
suited to our conditions.

1133
01:21:20,062 --> 01:21:23,646
What's the point?
As if you would live here!

1134
01:21:24,437 --> 01:21:26,646
You're lucky. Your mother
was a U.K. citizen.

1135
01:21:27,229 --> 01:21:28,521
You're so negative!

1136
01:21:28,979 --> 01:21:30,521
He has no affection for India.

1137
01:21:30,729 --> 01:21:32,771
I'd love India too if I lived abroad.

1138
01:21:34,271 --> 01:21:36,521
Non-resident Indians love India
the most!

1139
01:21:37,229 --> 01:21:39,437
That's why they live abroad.

1140
01:22:02,729 --> 01:22:04,312
<i>And you know, the worst part is</i>

1141
01:22:04,396 --> 01:22:07,354
<i>she's not even making my sister
do anything. And it's her...</i>

1142
01:22:07,687 --> 01:22:09,979
<i>Hello? Are you listening?</i>

1143
01:22:12,604 --> 01:22:15,896
When is Indu's wedding?
In November, right?

1144
01:22:16,521 --> 01:22:17,521
<i>November 11.</i>

1145
01:22:18,271 --> 01:22:19,604
- I'll be there.
- What!

1146
01:22:20,229 --> 01:22:21,437
What's come over you?

1147
01:22:22,062 --> 01:22:25,812
Why not? What's the big deal
if I come?

1148
01:22:26,812 --> 01:22:27,854
Commitment!

1149
01:22:28,146 --> 01:22:30,646
That's fine. People make commitments.

1150
01:22:31,271 --> 01:22:32,354
Look at my grandpa.

1151
01:22:33,104 --> 01:22:36,771
He's been committed to something
for 78 years. He won't change.

1152
01:22:38,562 --> 01:22:40,812
And I can't stay committed
for seven weeks.

1153
01:22:41,312 --> 01:22:44,187
Wow! You actually said that.

1154
01:22:44,562 --> 01:22:45,646
I bore myself now.

1155
01:22:46,729 --> 01:22:50,062
Let's start again. I'll come
to your sister's wedding.

1156
01:22:50,687 --> 01:22:55,521
My whole family will be there.
And friends and everyone.

1157
01:22:56,187 --> 01:22:57,104
So what?

1158
01:22:58,062 --> 01:22:59,687
They'll ask who you are.

1159
01:23:00,562 --> 01:23:01,437
Your boyfriend.

1160
01:23:01,562 --> 01:23:02,437
<i>Really?</i>

1161
01:23:02,729 --> 01:23:03,729
<i>Yeah!</i>

1162
01:23:04,104 --> 01:23:06,146
<i>Then you'll disappear again.</i>

1163
01:23:06,687 --> 01:23:08,979
<i>How do I explain where
my boyfriend's gone?</i>

1164
01:23:09,229 --> 01:23:11,021
I'm not going to disappear.

1165
01:23:11,521 --> 01:23:15,521
Being scared of relationships

1166
01:23:16,146 --> 01:23:17,562
quitting job after job

1167
01:23:18,437 --> 01:23:19,687
changing apartments

1168
01:23:20,396 --> 01:23:21,437
that's over.

1169
01:23:22,896 --> 01:23:25,479
I want to be focused,
like grandpa.

1170
01:23:25,979 --> 01:23:29,729
Very nice!
This is your social experiment.

1171
01:23:29,812 --> 01:23:32,479
Then you'll take a U-turn
and I'll be trapped.

1172
01:23:32,604 --> 01:23:33,479
No, thanks.

1173
01:23:34,896 --> 01:23:35,937
Now you're scared!

1174
01:23:37,687 --> 01:23:38,521
Huh?

1175
01:23:38,937 --> 01:23:42,354
Now that I'm committing myself,
you're backing off.

1176
01:23:44,979 --> 01:23:47,187
What's all the hurry?

1177
01:23:48,271 --> 01:23:49,729
We all have the same problem.

1178
01:23:51,229 --> 01:23:52,604
<i>Commitment.</i>

1179
01:23:56,562 --> 01:23:58,562
What's written here, sir?

1180
01:23:58,854 --> 01:24:01,646
What language is this?
I can't read it.

1181
01:24:05,562 --> 01:24:08,604
Listen to him shouting!
Who shouts like that!

1182
01:24:08,729 --> 01:24:10,812
Don't ever mention that tattoo.

1183
01:24:11,021 --> 01:24:14,562
I was briefed before my wedding.
I forgot to tell you.

1184
01:24:15,479 --> 01:24:18,562
It's not good for his health
to shout so loudly.

1185
01:24:18,646 --> 01:24:19,812
- Yes.
- What's it to me?

1186
01:24:25,437 --> 01:24:27,812
You love terrorizing people!

1187
01:24:28,104 --> 01:24:28,979
Right?

1188
01:24:30,979 --> 01:24:32,021
"Queen Dilfareb."

1189
01:24:34,437 --> 01:24:36,187
Isn't that what your tattoo says?

1190
01:24:37,146 --> 01:24:38,271
Nice name.

1191
01:24:39,187 --> 01:24:40,437
Why can't one say it?

1192
01:24:41,604 --> 01:24:43,687
Queen Dilfareb.

1193
01:24:45,187 --> 01:24:48,146
I’m wondering how Dilfareb
could be anyone’s name.

1194
01:24:48,312 --> 01:24:49,354
- Nivi.
- Yes?

1195
01:24:50,896 --> 01:24:52,271
Queen is possible.

1196
01:24:53,354 --> 01:24:56,437
Did you know a queen?
Was there a "queen"?

1197
01:24:58,437 --> 01:24:59,271
Was there?

1198
01:25:00,854 --> 01:25:02,062
Tell me, Grandpa.

1199
01:25:02,354 --> 01:25:03,812
Tell us. Come on.

1200
01:25:05,771 --> 01:25:07,562
Does the rocket have petrol?

1201
01:25:09,812 --> 01:25:10,687
What?

1202
01:25:11,562 --> 01:25:13,479
Does the rocket have petrol?

1203
01:25:13,562 --> 01:25:15,229
Where do you want to go?

1204
01:25:15,312 --> 01:25:16,771
To the moon.

1205
01:25:16,896 --> 01:25:20,146
The moon is vast.
Any particular destination?

1206
01:25:23,104 --> 01:25:23,937
College?

1207
01:25:24,937 --> 01:25:26,437
Government College?

1208
01:25:26,604 --> 01:25:27,896
A church!

1209
01:25:29,104 --> 01:25:30,562
Where exactly is this church?

1210
01:25:30,646 --> 01:25:35,021
Exit the rocket. It's on the right.

1211
01:25:35,521 --> 01:25:37,062
Last time you said on the left.

1212
01:25:37,146 --> 01:25:39,312
That's because I came from the other side.

1213
01:25:40,271 --> 01:25:42,187
From Antarctica.

1214
01:25:42,271 --> 01:25:46,312
Antarctica was a part of India then.

1215
01:25:48,562 --> 01:25:52,479
Why do you want to go to church?

1216
01:25:53,812 --> 01:25:56,896
When the wind blows through the fields

1217
01:25:58,187 --> 01:26:01,729
the Martians make a noise.

1218
01:26:02,146 --> 01:26:04,104
<i>Ho, ho, ho.</i>

1219
01:26:05,312 --> 01:26:08,479
- They don't walk alone.
- They move in groups?

1220
01:26:08,562 --> 01:26:09,854
Nivi, let him be.

1221
01:26:11,771 --> 01:26:13,396
Where is Queen Dilfareb?

1222
01:26:15,437 --> 01:26:16,521
In the church.

1223
01:26:17,479 --> 01:26:20,062
I see. You want to meet her there?

1224
01:26:22,229 --> 01:26:24,729
Why? Any special reason?

1225
01:26:28,437 --> 01:26:30,437
I know you want to meet her.

1226
01:26:30,646 --> 01:26:31,646
But why?

1227
01:26:34,979 --> 01:26:37,146
<i>You always say such foolish things!</i>

1228
01:27:55,437 --> 01:27:56,271
The door.

1229
01:27:57,646 --> 01:27:58,562
Close it.

1230
01:28:05,562 --> 01:28:06,562
Something wrong?

1231
01:28:12,937 --> 01:28:16,271
You didn't want trouble.

1232
01:28:17,229 --> 01:28:18,979
You said I should take it back.

1233
01:28:19,896 --> 01:28:22,229
I cannot take it anymore, Keenu.

1234
01:28:23,104 --> 01:28:24,354
I can't sleep at night.

1235
01:28:25,812 --> 01:28:27,229
I feel so restless.

1236
01:28:28,437 --> 01:28:29,687
What's happening to me?

1237
01:28:29,937 --> 01:28:31,146
That's the way it is.

1238
01:28:32,187 --> 01:28:33,062
What?

1239
01:28:34,271 --> 01:28:35,354
What we're feeling.

1240
01:28:40,396 --> 01:28:42,437
Does everyone feel the same?

1241
01:28:42,521 --> 01:28:43,479
Yes.

1242
01:28:43,896 --> 01:28:45,229
Then why do they do it?

1243
01:28:47,562 --> 01:28:52,604
Girls long to fall in love.
To feel like this?

1244
01:28:55,812 --> 01:28:58,812
It's a curse! I don't want it.

1245
01:28:59,312 --> 01:29:00,729
I came to make it stop.

1246
01:29:01,437 --> 01:29:02,354
How?

1247
01:29:02,604 --> 01:29:03,479
Look at this.

1248
01:29:04,687 --> 01:29:06,354
I can't see anything.

1249
01:29:06,562 --> 01:29:08,229
There's no light. Look...

1250
01:29:08,437 --> 01:29:09,312
Jiya!

1251
01:29:15,937 --> 01:29:17,437
- Jiya!
- Yes, Father?

1252
01:29:19,687 --> 01:29:21,521
What on earth happened?

1253
01:29:21,604 --> 01:29:22,437
Come out.

1254
01:29:49,521 --> 01:29:50,396
Grewal?

1255
01:29:51,729 --> 01:29:52,646
Good evening, sir.

1256
01:29:53,229 --> 01:29:57,729
What the hell's going on?
Have you no shame?

1257
01:29:58,521 --> 01:30:01,521
Is this how we raised you?
Despicable behavior!

1258
01:30:02,229 --> 01:30:04,229
You've lost your mind!

1259
01:30:06,021 --> 01:30:08,479
I know it looks like we're doing
something wrong.

1260
01:30:10,187 --> 01:30:12,604
We're meeting like thieves

1261
01:30:13,479 --> 01:30:18,729
because circumstances won't let us
meet out in the open.

1262
01:30:20,021 --> 01:30:21,104
That's why

1263
01:30:21,646 --> 01:30:25,479
in the middle of the night,
risking great danger, he came here...

1264
01:30:27,812 --> 01:30:28,729
for my sake.

1265
01:30:30,104 --> 01:30:31,854
To make me feel less tormented.

1266
01:30:33,021 --> 01:30:35,437
I am not ashamed.

1267
01:30:36,146 --> 01:30:37,646
You shameless girl!

1268
01:30:37,812 --> 01:30:39,354
Are you on drugs?

1269
01:30:40,104 --> 01:30:41,062
Get out!

1270
01:30:41,562 --> 01:30:43,062
Uncle, let go.

1271
01:30:43,646 --> 01:30:45,479
I can't believe my eyes.

1272
01:30:45,562 --> 01:30:46,812
What has come over her?

1273
01:30:47,479 --> 01:30:50,396
One slap and your audacity
will vanish!

1274
01:30:51,646 --> 01:30:52,521
Jiya!

1275
01:30:54,521 --> 01:30:55,396
Let's go.

1276
01:30:57,646 --> 01:30:59,646
- Jiya!
- Hey!

1277
01:31:00,687 --> 01:31:02,312
- Stop!
- Are we running away?

1278
01:31:03,146 --> 01:31:04,229
Bend down.

1279
01:31:04,312 --> 01:31:05,396
- Jiya!
- This way.

1280
01:31:07,771 --> 01:31:08,646
Come.

1281
01:31:08,729 --> 01:31:10,479
- Kamran, look over there.
- Yes, sir.

1282
01:31:10,562 --> 01:31:12,021
- Give me the lantern.
- Jiya.

1283
01:31:12,479 --> 01:31:13,354
Come, Saifuddin.

1284
01:31:14,729 --> 01:31:15,729
Jiya!

1285
01:31:16,021 --> 01:31:18,646
You said I'd forget you
when the time came.

1286
01:31:19,562 --> 01:31:21,646
Your name would rub off.

1287
01:31:22,729 --> 01:31:23,646
Look!

1288
01:31:27,146 --> 01:31:29,229
Indelible ink.

1289
01:31:30,104 --> 01:31:31,229
It'll never fade!

1290
01:31:37,146 --> 01:31:39,229
They're here. Here.

1291
01:32:01,187 --> 01:32:02,354
My cycle.

1292
01:32:46,646 --> 01:32:49,937
My grandfather migrated
from Kolkata to Dhaka

1293
01:32:50,812 --> 01:32:52,896
in 1947.

1294
01:32:53,354 --> 01:32:56,562
Twenty years later, we moved
to Lahore.

1295
01:32:57,146 --> 01:32:58,312
Now we belong here.

1296
01:32:59,146 --> 01:33:00,312
And we belong here.

1297
01:33:01,312 --> 01:33:06,896
Adil, you know those pictures you sent
of Sargodha?

1298
01:33:06,979 --> 01:33:10,229
- The old family-style pictures.
- They were amazing.

1299
01:33:10,312 --> 01:33:13,562
They might jog grandpa's memory.
He might remember something.

1300
01:33:13,896 --> 01:33:17,646
We have many photos like them.
We can mail them to you.

1301
01:33:18,146 --> 01:33:20,437
Awesome. I have an idea.

1302
01:33:23,896 --> 01:33:24,854
Lights.

1303
01:33:28,229 --> 01:33:29,437
Showtime, Grandpa!

1304
01:34:07,437 --> 01:34:09,521
Is this her? Queen Dilfareb?

1305
01:34:23,812 --> 01:34:26,354
Sir? Sir?

1306
01:34:28,729 --> 01:34:29,687
What is it?

1307
01:34:30,229 --> 01:34:31,437
Nurse, what's going on?

1308
01:34:32,562 --> 01:34:33,521
What is it?

1309
01:34:34,312 --> 01:34:36,562
- Grandpa.
- Shit!

1310
01:34:36,896 --> 01:34:38,396
Is he breathing?

1311
01:34:39,396 --> 01:34:40,271
Is he?

1312
01:34:43,771 --> 01:34:44,937
Hurry up.

1313
01:34:50,854 --> 01:34:51,937
Jasmer!

1314
01:34:52,104 --> 01:34:54,146
Burn the farms!

1315
01:34:54,437 --> 01:34:56,521
Jasmer! Jasmer!

1316
01:34:56,729 --> 01:34:58,229
- Uncle.
- Hurry up!

1317
01:34:58,396 --> 01:34:59,979
- There's fire.
- Hurry up.

1318
01:35:02,521 --> 01:35:04,271
Jazz! Pay attention!

1319
01:35:04,562 --> 01:35:06,479
Go now!

1320
01:35:06,562 --> 01:35:08,437
They're coming. They're here.

1321
01:35:08,562 --> 01:35:09,479
What happened?

1322
01:35:09,562 --> 01:35:13,229
I'm sorry, sir.
Come home with me now.

1323
01:35:17,437 --> 01:35:20,104
- The phone's dead.
- They must've cut the line.

1324
01:35:20,187 --> 01:35:22,479
Hello, Dr. Gaur. An emergency.

1325
01:35:22,937 --> 01:35:25,604
Father isn't moving.
His heart rate has dropped.

1326
01:35:26,812 --> 01:35:28,937
Happy now? Satisfied?

1327
01:35:32,396 --> 01:35:34,479
Keenu!

1328
01:35:37,937 --> 01:35:39,146
What's going on, Mother?

1329
01:35:39,229 --> 01:35:43,562
What's going on? What are we doing?

1330
01:35:44,146 --> 01:35:47,271
- Get the money from the safe.
- But we're not leaving for good.

1331
01:35:47,437 --> 01:35:48,687
I will not go.

1332
01:35:49,937 --> 01:35:50,854
I will not go.

1333
01:35:50,937 --> 01:35:53,187
- Mother, we're not leaving.
- Come on, Keenu.

1334
01:35:56,104 --> 01:35:58,229
Let's go. Hey, what are you doing?

1335
01:35:58,562 --> 01:36:00,812
Sir, what are you doing?

1336
01:36:02,729 --> 01:36:05,271
O Lord!

1337
01:36:07,146 --> 01:36:09,479
I am not going.

1338
01:36:09,854 --> 01:36:11,146
Hurry up.

1339
01:36:15,521 --> 01:36:17,604
I'm not leaving.

1340
01:36:18,354 --> 01:36:20,437
I'm not leaving.

1341
01:36:20,854 --> 01:36:23,187
Not going, I promised.

1342
01:36:54,896 --> 01:36:58,604
So often terror and violence
charged into homes for people

1343
01:36:58,937 --> 01:37:00,271
to realize their presence.

1344
01:37:00,437 --> 01:37:01,854
The Amritsar train arrives
in the morning.

1345
01:37:02,021 --> 01:37:04,812
No one knew what would
happen next.

1346
01:37:04,979 --> 01:37:07,812
- They've set fire to the fields.
- They've gone. They won't come back.

1347
01:37:07,937 --> 01:37:11,396
In those days news spread so slowly.

1348
01:37:13,146 --> 01:37:15,312
People today cannot imagine.

1349
01:37:16,729 --> 01:37:19,896
I beg you, Jasmer.
Sir, please come with me.

1350
01:37:19,979 --> 01:37:22,979
Muzaffar, they'll kill you.
They already call you a traitor.

1351
01:37:23,062 --> 01:37:24,187
Should I let you die?

1352
01:37:24,271 --> 01:37:27,187
We'll hide in the fields.
It'll be fine in the morning.

1353
01:37:29,062 --> 01:37:30,479
Nothing will be fine.

1354
01:37:30,562 --> 01:37:32,521
Let's keep moving.

1355
01:37:32,687 --> 01:37:34,771
And the women? What will they do?

1356
01:37:34,979 --> 01:37:38,354
We'll follow you. We're staying with you.

1357
01:37:38,437 --> 01:37:40,021
What if the rioters catch us?

1358
01:37:42,104 --> 01:37:45,062
You heard what they did to women
in Rawalpindi?

1359
01:37:45,354 --> 01:37:47,687
Muzaffar, take the women with you.

1360
01:37:47,896 --> 01:37:49,312
- Keep them safe.
- Yes.

1361
01:37:49,396 --> 01:37:50,271
This is wrong.

1362
01:37:50,354 --> 01:37:53,229
Father, if he shelters them,
his family will be in danger.

1363
01:37:53,312 --> 01:37:56,396
Son, no one can determine their religion
by looking at women.

1364
01:37:56,562 --> 01:37:57,771
No, don't.

1365
01:37:58,021 --> 01:38:00,312
We can hide them in the storehouse
for tonight.

1366
01:38:00,396 --> 01:38:01,812
Even our servants won't know.

1367
01:38:02,104 --> 01:38:04,312
We're going with you, sir.

1368
01:38:04,812 --> 01:38:06,687
Don't separate us.

1369
01:38:07,354 --> 01:38:10,396
Let's stick together.

1370
01:38:10,812 --> 01:38:14,604
Hey, don't separate us! This is a mistake.

1371
01:38:15,021 --> 01:38:15,979
A big mistake!

1372
01:38:34,396 --> 01:38:37,187
When did you arrive from England?

1373
01:38:38,604 --> 01:38:39,979
Two weeks ago.

1374
01:38:41,062 --> 01:38:42,354
Thank God.

1375
01:38:42,521 --> 01:38:45,687
- How are you feeling?
- Are you married?

1376
01:38:47,271 --> 01:38:48,354
No. No chance.

1377
01:38:48,479 --> 01:38:52,604
Don't get married.

1378
01:38:53,854 --> 01:38:55,604
Ever.

1379
01:38:55,771 --> 01:38:57,521
Never get married.

1380
01:38:57,604 --> 01:38:59,646
Come, Nivi, let's have lunch.

1381
01:38:59,729 --> 01:39:01,021
Why, Grandpa?

1382
01:39:01,146 --> 01:39:04,479
The women in our family die.

1383
01:39:05,896 --> 01:39:07,812
We've been cursed.

1384
01:39:08,562 --> 01:39:10,062
Come on, let's go.

1385
01:39:10,396 --> 01:39:12,437
Shall we eat in or out?

1386
01:39:12,646 --> 01:39:13,979
What does he mean?

1387
01:39:14,187 --> 01:39:16,396
He's lost his mind. You know that.

1388
01:39:16,479 --> 01:39:20,604
He said they'd bring innocent girls here

1389
01:39:21,062 --> 01:39:23,312
and kill them.

1390
01:39:25,187 --> 01:39:26,562
It's wrong.

1391
01:39:27,104 --> 01:39:30,562
It must stop. It has to stop.

1392
01:39:32,604 --> 01:39:33,896
It must be stopped.

1393
01:39:34,687 --> 01:39:36,604
They kill them.

1394
01:39:36,687 --> 01:39:39,062
Who kills them, Grandpa? The Martians?

1395
01:39:39,687 --> 01:39:41,354
The people here.

1396
01:39:43,062 --> 01:39:45,354
In this house.

1397
01:39:47,021 --> 01:39:49,312
Stop listening to this nonsense.
Come!

1398
01:39:49,396 --> 01:39:52,146
He's the one!

1399
01:39:52,312 --> 01:39:55,354
I told him over and over again
not to marry.

1400
01:39:55,437 --> 01:39:57,771
He didn't listen. He's a murderer.

1401
01:39:57,896 --> 01:40:00,396
A murderer.

1402
01:40:00,562 --> 01:40:03,187
- Father, I beg you.
- Why did you get married?

1403
01:40:03,687 --> 01:40:06,896
She was someone's child.

1404
01:40:07,687 --> 01:40:12,062
They snatched her away.

1405
01:40:12,979 --> 01:40:14,854
Is he talking about mom?

1406
01:40:14,937 --> 01:40:18,479
He finished her off, killed her.

1407
01:40:18,562 --> 01:40:20,979
You think you'll be spared.

1408
01:40:21,396 --> 01:40:24,562
Everyone will be punished.
No one will be spared.

1409
01:40:24,854 --> 01:40:30,354
What we did to them will be done to us.

1410
01:40:33,979 --> 01:40:39,812
We tortured that innocent soul.
We killed her.

1411
01:40:40,354 --> 01:40:43,521
We are responsible. Now we must face it.

1412
01:40:43,896 --> 01:40:46,479
<i>We killed her with our own hands.</i>

1413
01:40:46,896 --> 01:40:49,854
<i>Strangled her.</i>

1414
01:40:50,604 --> 01:40:53,187
<i>We shall not be shown pity.</i>

1415
01:40:53,562 --> 01:40:56,771
<i>No one will protect us</i>

1416
01:40:57,479 --> 01:41:01,354
<i>because we did not protect them.</i>

1417
01:41:02,271 --> 01:41:04,604
<i>We could have.</i>

1418
01:41:04,687 --> 01:41:07,396
<i>We could have, but we didn't.</i>

1419
01:41:07,521 --> 01:41:08,896
<i>We let it happen.</i>

1420
01:41:08,979 --> 01:41:11,312
<i>There'll be no hearing now.</i>

1421
01:41:11,521 --> 01:41:14,396
<i>We shall reap as we have sown.</i>

1422
01:41:14,479 --> 01:41:16,396
Justice will be done.

1423
01:41:16,521 --> 01:41:19,437
Justice will be done.

1424
01:41:19,854 --> 01:41:21,062
Brother!

1425
01:41:24,812 --> 01:41:25,729
Yes.

1426
01:41:26,396 --> 01:41:28,854
We... We'll have to pay.

1427
01:41:29,979 --> 01:41:33,521
We should've stopped them.

1428
01:41:33,646 --> 01:41:34,687
Brother.

1429
01:41:35,604 --> 01:41:37,396
Don't look back.

1430
01:41:37,812 --> 01:41:39,062
Brother.

1431
01:41:39,146 --> 01:41:40,771
There's nothing left there.

1432
01:41:42,562 --> 01:41:44,271
You've forgotten?

1433
01:41:52,604 --> 01:41:56,146
Brother!

1434
01:42:03,104 --> 01:42:04,187
Brother!

1435
01:42:05,354 --> 01:42:06,687
Curse?

1436
01:42:09,146 --> 01:42:12,479
It's nonsense! There's no truth to it.

1437
01:42:15,271 --> 01:42:16,979
I'm sure. But what is this curse?

1438
01:42:21,521 --> 01:42:22,521
Dad?

1439
01:42:35,521 --> 01:42:41,312
Trains from Pakistan would arrive
at Amritsar station daily.

1440
01:42:43,146 --> 01:42:46,896
Sardar Jasmer Singh, my grandpa...

1441
01:42:50,146 --> 01:42:51,937
went to Amritsar station

1442
01:42:53,354 --> 01:42:55,312
every day for 18 years.

1443
01:42:57,562 --> 01:43:01,312
He had hoped the women of our family
would be on the train.

1444
01:43:07,062 --> 01:43:09,146
Will you forgive me, son?

1445
01:43:10,312 --> 01:43:11,354
Forgive me.

1446
01:43:11,521 --> 01:43:13,479
<i>I saw my grandfather
on his deathbed.</i>

1447
01:43:13,646 --> 01:43:15,104
I told you to get married.

1448
01:43:15,187 --> 01:43:18,146
<i>Grandpa talked about the curse.</i>

1449
01:43:20,562 --> 01:43:22,521
And your wife passed away.

1450
01:43:23,104 --> 01:43:26,396
My mother died a few years earlier.

1451
01:43:27,687 --> 01:43:32,437
Son, we could not protect
the women in our family...

1452
01:43:35,562 --> 01:43:37,187
so they put a curse on us...

1453
01:43:39,604 --> 01:43:41,771
that no women in this family
would survive.

1454
01:43:42,771 --> 01:43:44,229
We could not protect them?

1455
01:43:47,687 --> 01:43:49,104
What happened to the women?

1456
01:43:50,479 --> 01:43:51,437
I don't know.

1457
01:43:52,354 --> 01:43:55,771
Ask father what's happened
to the women.

1458
01:43:56,437 --> 01:44:00,021
<i>- My sister and I used to chat as kids.</i>
- No. I won't ask.

1459
01:44:00,354 --> 01:44:04,271
- Father won't hit you, you're a girl.
<i>- Angad was very young then.</i>

1460
01:44:04,354 --> 01:44:06,437
No, I won't ask.

1461
01:44:10,312 --> 01:44:11,646
Brother.

1462
01:44:11,896 --> 01:44:14,146
Must we go to the factory today?

1463
01:44:15,062 --> 01:44:16,604
The order has to be delivered.

1464
01:44:17,812 --> 01:44:19,104
We must check.

1465
01:44:19,437 --> 01:44:20,896
Don't, Brother.

1466
01:44:21,187 --> 01:44:22,479
Your father has died.

1467
01:44:22,562 --> 01:44:25,979
You want to mourn? To cry?

1468
01:44:27,854 --> 01:44:30,771
Is that the first dead body
you've ever seen?

1469
01:44:31,187 --> 01:44:32,146
Brother...

1470
01:44:34,979 --> 01:44:36,687
We're refugees, Pali.

1471
01:44:37,187 --> 01:44:39,437
We can't afford to mourn.

1472
01:44:42,104 --> 01:44:43,771
The funeral is in four hours.

1473
01:44:45,104 --> 01:44:46,479
I'll be back by then.

1474
01:44:47,896 --> 01:44:50,312
Let's go. We're getting late.

1475
01:44:51,312 --> 01:44:53,021
We're getting late.

1476
01:44:59,979 --> 01:45:00,979
<i>My sister...</i>

1477
01:45:03,687 --> 01:45:04,896
<i>passed away</i>

1478
01:45:06,896 --> 01:45:08,396
<i>when she was 25.</i>

1479
01:45:10,312 --> 01:45:13,479
<i>And then your mother, Teji.</i>

1480
01:45:44,687 --> 01:45:45,896
Running away again?

1481
01:45:47,729 --> 01:45:49,437
Leaving another job unfinished?

1482
01:45:51,729 --> 01:45:55,354
It's best I go. I am a danger
to him here.

1483
01:45:56,687 --> 01:45:58,479
I almost killed Grandpa.

1484
01:46:03,479 --> 01:46:05,896
Do you believe grandpa
is happy to be alive?

1485
01:46:08,771 --> 01:46:12,229
He's in constant pain,
both physically and mentally.

1486
01:46:12,312 --> 01:46:15,021
Every breath he takes is painful.
Is he enjoying it?

1487
01:46:16,187 --> 01:46:18,062
His stubbornness keeps him alive.

1488
01:46:18,187 --> 01:46:20,062
He's tolerating life

1489
01:46:20,896 --> 01:46:22,687
because there's a mystery to solve.

1490
01:46:23,312 --> 01:46:25,687
His unfinished business...
your theory, right?

1491
01:46:26,687 --> 01:46:27,979
It's not a theory, Dad.

1492
01:46:29,979 --> 01:46:32,062
A theory is backed by scientific evidence

1493
01:46:32,687 --> 01:46:35,729
this is mere fantasy.

1494
01:46:38,687 --> 01:46:42,437
There's a girl he's remembering
in his final moments.

1495
01:46:44,896 --> 01:46:46,896
He must remember many women.

1496
01:46:50,479 --> 01:46:52,271
All the women in his family.

1497
01:46:56,396 --> 01:47:00,396
Women suffered double trauma
during Partition, didn't they?

1498
01:47:02,687 --> 01:47:04,729
I can't imagine what they had to endure.

1499
01:47:05,187 --> 01:47:06,687
And at whose mercy they were.

1500
01:47:08,979 --> 01:47:11,437
Shit. I don't even want to think about it.

1501
01:47:12,354 --> 01:47:13,271
Why?

1502
01:47:15,562 --> 01:47:17,687
What's the problem in thinking about them?

1503
01:47:18,521 --> 01:47:21,562
We didn't know them.
So how can we feel anything?

1504
01:47:22,646 --> 01:47:23,896
But dad knew them.

1505
01:47:25,187 --> 01:47:26,687
He must think about them.

1506
01:47:27,687 --> 01:47:29,562
He must've been worried all his life

1507
01:47:30,062 --> 01:47:34,062
about what happened to the women,
to his mother, his sister

1508
01:47:34,729 --> 01:47:36,229
who took them and...

1509
01:47:36,979 --> 01:47:38,687
What can I do about it, Dad?

1510
01:47:40,146 --> 01:47:41,854
How can I change the past?

1511
01:47:46,187 --> 01:47:47,396
You can't do anything?

1512
01:47:51,562 --> 01:47:52,479
Okay.

1513
01:47:53,354 --> 01:47:56,021
If you can't do anything,
then no one can.

1514
01:47:56,729 --> 01:47:57,896
He chose you.

1515
01:47:58,646 --> 01:48:00,396
If you can't do anything

1516
01:48:01,854 --> 01:48:03,687
then he'll die tormented.

1517
01:48:06,271 --> 01:48:08,062
He'll die in pain, the way he lived.

1518
01:48:35,062 --> 01:48:35,979
Allow me.

1519
01:48:37,396 --> 01:48:39,062
Come, Grandpa. I'll take you out.

1520
01:48:42,979 --> 01:48:44,396
See that tree?

1521
01:48:48,187 --> 01:48:50,271
Teji planted it.

1522
01:48:51,812 --> 01:48:55,187
Remember Teji? Your favourite.

1523
01:48:56,854 --> 01:48:57,896
My mother.

1524
01:48:59,562 --> 01:49:01,104
She's with God now.

1525
01:49:04,896 --> 01:49:06,396
Where's your mother, Grandpa?

1526
01:49:06,604 --> 01:49:07,812
Not again!

1527
01:49:10,104 --> 01:49:12,187
- You must've had sisters too.
- Listen...

1528
01:49:16,187 --> 01:49:19,354
Where are all the women in your family?

1529
01:49:21,687 --> 01:49:22,979
Mexico.

1530
01:49:23,979 --> 01:49:24,896
Mexico?

1531
01:49:26,521 --> 01:49:28,604
They rode there.

1532
01:49:30,521 --> 01:49:33,979
- Why Mexico?
- There was a fire.

1533
01:49:35,562 --> 01:49:38,812
Here and there too.

1534
01:49:39,979 --> 01:49:41,771
They were taken away.

1535
01:49:43,396 --> 01:49:46,229
Who took them? The Martians?

1536
01:49:46,687 --> 01:49:52,479
The Martians have small eyes...

1537
01:49:52,562 --> 01:49:56,604
The women, Grandpa.
Were you there when they were taken?

1538
01:49:57,354 --> 01:50:03,354
I was... the opening bowler.

1539
01:50:03,937 --> 01:50:05,104
Grandpa.

1540
01:50:05,562 --> 01:50:12,521
The people of Mars attacked the moon.

1541
01:50:14,062 --> 01:50:16,146
They set it on fire.

1542
01:50:16,771 --> 01:50:18,979
Did the Martians set the fields
on fire too?

1543
01:50:20,062 --> 01:50:22,479
Blue skin.

1544
01:50:23,937 --> 01:50:26,396
Were you there? In the fields?

1545
01:50:27,979 --> 01:50:31,521
I was underwater.

1546
01:50:32,312 --> 01:50:33,187
Underwater?

1547
01:50:33,354 --> 01:50:37,562
<i>Under the surface of the water.</i>

1548
01:50:38,062 --> 01:50:41,271
Kapil Dev was with me.

1549
01:50:43,271 --> 01:50:47,896
His hand started hurting

1550
01:50:49,146 --> 01:50:51,396
so he requested me...

1551
01:50:52,854 --> 01:50:56,521
"Please open the bowling for India."

1552
01:51:02,437 --> 01:51:07,146
- Did you find anyone?
- No one, sir.

1553
01:51:09,396 --> 01:51:10,896
Brother.

1554
01:51:12,937 --> 01:51:14,396
My hand is hurting.

1555
01:51:15,771 --> 01:51:19,646
Don't think about it, Pali.
Think about something else.

1556
01:51:21,979 --> 01:51:23,771
What are you thinking, Brother?

1557
01:51:25,896 --> 01:51:27,979
Think of something...

1558
01:51:29,271 --> 01:51:30,437
you like.

1559
01:51:32,521 --> 01:51:34,104
And hold my shoulder tightly.

1560
01:51:54,646 --> 01:51:56,729
Hurry, keep down.

1561
01:51:57,646 --> 01:51:59,062
I know a shortcut!

1562
01:51:59,146 --> 01:52:01,937
- I know a shortcut.
- Let the dawn break.

1563
01:52:02,021 --> 01:52:03,146
- No.
- Wait until dawn.

1564
01:52:03,229 --> 01:52:06,104
Then there'll be too much light.
We must move in the dark.

1565
01:52:06,937 --> 01:52:09,104
Father, I know a place
where we can hide.

1566
01:52:09,562 --> 01:52:14,521
Standing in the dark, she's wearing
a white dress.

1567
01:52:14,604 --> 01:52:15,687
Who's in white?

1568
01:52:17,146 --> 01:52:18,187
Who's that?

1569
01:52:19,187 --> 01:52:20,146
Ji...?

1570
01:52:53,937 --> 01:52:54,979
I am not leaving.

1571
01:53:06,771 --> 01:53:07,979
I'm not leaving.

1572
01:53:35,979 --> 01:53:37,729
Keenu, come here.

1573
01:53:40,604 --> 01:53:41,771
Keenu, run!

1574
01:53:43,687 --> 01:53:45,396
Keenu, run!

1575
01:53:47,812 --> 01:53:49,896
Stop! Don't.

1576
01:53:53,312 --> 01:53:55,187
<i>It was a mistake.</i>

1577
01:53:56,437 --> 01:54:01,312
<i>Sparks flew from Churchill's cigar.</i>

1578
01:54:01,979 --> 01:54:05,229
He bombed the civilians.

1579
01:54:05,979 --> 01:54:10,812
He may sound lucid sometimes,
but he's becoming increasingly confused.

1580
01:54:12,187 --> 01:54:14,104
That is not a good sign.

1581
01:54:14,187 --> 01:54:17,312
The Russian army is coming.

1582
01:54:17,979 --> 01:54:20,312
The war will start again.

1583
01:54:21,437 --> 01:54:24,937
Two pieces will float away separately.

1584
01:54:26,104 --> 01:54:28,896
It was a mistake.

1585
01:54:30,271 --> 01:54:32,312
Why don't you give him
another injection?

1586
01:54:32,479 --> 01:54:35,062
He's already had the highest
permissible dose.

1587
01:54:35,187 --> 01:54:36,729
We cannot increase it.

1588
01:54:36,896 --> 01:54:38,354
His heart will stop.

1589
01:54:48,229 --> 01:54:50,354
<i>The earring.</i>

1590
01:54:50,687 --> 01:54:52,771
<i>It was there.</i>

1591
01:54:53,146 --> 01:54:58,396
But it's tainted now.

1592
01:55:07,312 --> 01:55:09,396
Inder, here!

1593
01:55:41,812 --> 01:55:44,062
Jump in the back.

1594
01:55:44,187 --> 01:55:45,896
No, no, not here.

1595
01:55:50,062 --> 01:55:52,146
Father!

1596
01:55:59,312 --> 01:56:01,396
Keenu, go ahead.

1597
01:56:07,937 --> 01:56:09,062
Brother.

1598
01:56:10,312 --> 01:56:11,354
Keenu.

1599
01:56:12,812 --> 01:56:14,437
- Lift him. Careful.
- Yes.

1600
01:56:18,104 --> 01:56:19,354
Come on.

1601
01:56:23,062 --> 01:56:25,146
O Lord!

1602
01:56:44,937 --> 01:56:46,271
Is anyone left?

1603
01:56:46,396 --> 01:56:48,479
Inder!

1604
01:56:50,312 --> 01:56:51,354
Come in, Father.

1605
01:56:51,437 --> 01:56:53,521
Keenu.

1606
01:57:00,521 --> 01:57:01,729
Keenu!

1607
01:57:02,229 --> 01:57:03,521
We'll return.

1608
01:57:03,812 --> 01:57:05,687
Your mother is here.
They're all here.

1609
01:57:05,771 --> 01:57:07,312
We'll come back.

1610
01:57:07,854 --> 01:57:09,229
We'll come back.

1611
01:58:11,062 --> 01:58:12,396
I am not leaving.

1612
01:58:16,062 --> 01:58:17,771
I will never leave you.

1613
01:58:20,896 --> 01:58:22,937
I will come back.

1614
01:58:25,979 --> 01:58:28,437
I will come back.

1615
01:58:42,396 --> 01:58:48,646
<i>This terrible separation!
Distances pulling us apart</i>

1616
01:58:49,896 --> 01:58:56,687
<i>When I look back, I see</i>

1617
01:58:57,146 --> 01:59:01,437
<i>A river of misery</i>

1618
01:59:04,479 --> 01:59:10,146
<i>A river of misery</i>

1619
01:59:13,896 --> 01:59:17,812
Look! We're here. We're in India.

1620
01:59:18,437 --> 01:59:21,229
<i>The language of the eyes</i>

1621
01:59:21,312 --> 01:59:22,146
Father...

1622
01:59:22,229 --> 01:59:25,104
<i>Understood by no one</i>

1623
01:59:25,646 --> 01:59:28,646
<i>Where do I wander?</i>

1624
01:59:29,271 --> 01:59:30,312
He's no more.

1625
01:59:30,396 --> 01:59:36,771
<i>I float away, no one knows where</i>

1626
01:59:37,271 --> 01:59:41,437
<i>I float away with the river</i>

1627
01:59:44,979 --> 01:59:48,771
<i>The story of five rivers</i>

1628
01:59:50,437 --> 01:59:53,562
<i>Wherever they flow</i>

1629
01:59:53,687 --> 01:59:57,979
<i>The language changes</i>

1630
01:59:58,562 --> 02:00:05,562
<i>But my name is attached to you</i>

1631
02:00:07,562 --> 02:00:14,437
<i>If you are not by my side</i>

1632
02:00:16,021 --> 02:00:22,396
<i>I have no name</i>

1633
02:00:34,937 --> 02:00:37,687
There is a photo studio in Sargodha.

1634
02:00:38,187 --> 02:00:39,187
Blitzkrieg.

1635
02:00:39,562 --> 02:00:42,354
All the photos your grandfather
reacted to

1636
02:00:42,437 --> 02:00:44,562
- are from that studio.
- Blitzkrieg?

1637
02:00:46,312 --> 02:00:48,396
- Grewals, from Sargodha?
- Yes.

1638
02:00:48,729 --> 02:00:50,854
They all left for India
during Partition

1639
02:00:50,937 --> 02:00:53,312
except for Inderjeet Grewal.

1640
02:00:53,396 --> 02:00:55,354
Inderjeet Grewal?

1641
02:00:55,437 --> 02:00:57,062
That's a name grandpa mentioned.

1642
02:00:57,146 --> 02:00:58,687
"Directed by Inderjeet Grewal."

1643
02:00:58,771 --> 02:01:01,312
Where's he? Inder.

1644
02:01:01,396 --> 02:01:02,521
Inder.

1645
02:01:06,146 --> 02:01:07,854
<i>He never left for some reason.</i>

1646
02:01:09,229 --> 02:01:12,687
<i>He lived in Sargodha for two years.
Then he died.</i>

1647
02:01:13,771 --> 02:01:16,354
He opened this studio with my father.

1648
02:01:16,771 --> 02:01:17,646
Really?

1649
02:01:17,729 --> 02:01:20,854
Many years later, two members
of his family visited from India.

1650
02:01:21,479 --> 02:01:24,396
There's a photo of them
with my father. Get it out!

1651
02:01:25,562 --> 02:01:27,687
The man in the middle
is Bishamber Kohli.

1652
02:01:27,771 --> 02:01:30,521
He stayed back in Sargodha
at the time of Partition.

1653
02:01:30,604 --> 02:01:32,479
And these two are our family members.

1654
02:01:32,854 --> 02:01:33,854
Grewal.

1655
02:01:34,187 --> 02:01:35,479
The photo isn't clear.

1656
02:01:36,479 --> 02:01:38,646
Did any of us go back after Partition?

1657
02:01:54,396 --> 02:01:55,479
Where are you going?

1658
02:01:57,146 --> 02:01:58,562
We want to go to Sargodha.

1659
02:01:58,646 --> 02:02:02,104
Are you crazy? Can't you see them?

1660
02:02:06,979 --> 02:02:09,979
We must get through. Our family
was left behind.

1661
02:02:10,396 --> 02:02:13,521
Forget the people who were left behind.
Go back!

1662
02:02:13,687 --> 02:02:16,562
How can we forget?
We must get through.

1663
02:02:17,979 --> 02:02:19,896
The border's going to close.

1664
02:02:19,979 --> 02:02:22,562
- Arrest them!
- What are you doing?

1665
02:02:22,687 --> 02:02:26,396
Our women are on the other side.
Must we abandon them?

1666
02:02:26,479 --> 02:02:29,187
Our daughters were left behind.
We must go back.

1667
02:02:30,146 --> 02:02:33,937
- I beg you, let us through.
- Father.

1668
02:02:34,187 --> 02:02:36,271
- Let us pass.
- Father.

1669
02:02:37,896 --> 02:02:39,812
People are ripping each other apart.

1670
02:02:40,979 --> 02:02:43,187
If you want to save anyone,
save yourselves!

1671
02:02:45,396 --> 02:02:48,687
- We must get through. We must go.
- Father...

1672
02:02:48,771 --> 02:02:50,437
God knows how they are.

1673
02:02:50,521 --> 02:02:53,271
Father, I'll go.

1674
02:02:54,479 --> 02:02:55,979
I'll go, Father.

1675
02:02:56,896 --> 02:02:59,062
I'll bring them all back.

1676
02:03:01,062 --> 02:03:03,271
Take care of yourself.

1677
02:03:07,187 --> 02:03:10,521
- Catch him!
- Catch him!

1678
02:03:12,979 --> 02:03:14,771
<i>There's a city</i>

1679
02:03:15,187 --> 02:03:16,854
<i>There are people</i>

1680
02:03:17,437 --> 02:03:19,729
<i>There's a crowd</i>

1681
02:03:19,854 --> 02:03:21,896
<i>There's work</i>

1682
02:03:21,979 --> 02:03:28,562
<i>There's soft sunlight
Evening too</i>

1683
02:03:28,687 --> 02:03:30,979
<i>And there's me</i>

1684
02:03:31,062 --> 02:03:33,354
<i>But no longer the same</i>

1685
02:03:33,437 --> 02:03:39,687
<i>No longer the same
Though they call me by my name</i>

1686
02:03:40,062 --> 02:03:44,104
<i>There's noise, there's worry</i>

1687
02:03:44,604 --> 02:03:46,937
<i>And there's me</i>

1688
02:03:49,229 --> 02:03:51,562
<i>But no longer the same</i>

1689
02:04:11,521 --> 02:04:15,562
<i>This is my reality</i>

1690
02:04:15,937 --> 02:04:19,979
<i>This is your reality</i>

1691
02:04:20,354 --> 02:04:25,271
<i>Do not forget love</i>

1692
02:04:25,354 --> 02:04:29,187
<i>In these times of hatred</i>

1693
02:04:29,312 --> 02:04:33,562
<i>The raised hand</i>

1694
02:04:33,812 --> 02:04:37,771
<i>The severed head</i>

1695
02:04:38,187 --> 02:04:40,354
<i>That was me too</i>

1696
02:04:40,521 --> 02:04:44,604
<i>That was you, just us humans</i>

1697
02:04:47,187 --> 02:04:50,812
<i>There's heartbeat, blood too</i>

1698
02:04:51,479 --> 02:04:55,437
<i>There's breath, body too</i>

1699
02:04:56,146 --> 02:05:02,854
<i>How strange it is to accept</i>

1700
02:05:02,937 --> 02:05:07,104
<i>Living a life like this</i>

1701
02:05:07,437 --> 02:05:11,521
<i>It is my name too</i>

1702
02:05:11,896 --> 02:05:16,062
<i>It is my face</i>

1703
02:05:16,271 --> 02:05:21,479
<i>I was once both</i>

1704
02:05:23,396 --> 02:05:25,979
<i>But no longer the same</i>

1705
02:05:53,187 --> 02:05:57,479
<i>You are the one
Who put the heart aside</i>

1706
02:05:57,562 --> 02:06:01,437
<i>You are the one
Who fills every pore with grief</i>

1707
02:06:02,104 --> 02:06:06,479
<i>You are the one
Who is like the severed branch</i>

1708
02:06:06,562 --> 02:06:10,687
<i>You are the one
Who proclaims love every day</i>

1709
02:06:10,979 --> 02:06:15,396
<i>You are the one
Who turns into a crowd</i>

1710
02:06:15,479 --> 02:06:19,896
<i>You are the one
Who gets seduced by greed</i>

1711
02:06:19,979 --> 02:06:24,437
<i>You are the one
Who wanders from city to city</i>

1712
02:06:24,521 --> 02:06:28,937
<i>You are the one
Who falls from grace every day</i>

1713
02:06:29,021 --> 02:06:33,396
<i>You are the one
Who is seen so clearly</i>

1714
02:06:33,521 --> 02:06:37,812
<i>You are the one
Who writes such sweet lies</i>

1715
02:06:37,937 --> 02:06:42,354
<i>You are the one
Who grows old every day</i>

1716
02:06:42,437 --> 02:06:47,937
<i>You are the one
Who lives yet dies</i>

1717
02:06:48,271 --> 02:06:49,771
It's my decision.

1718
02:06:51,562 --> 02:06:53,646
I have chosen to take the risk.

1719
02:06:54,521 --> 02:06:58,521
<i>How can I express my pain?</i>

1720
02:06:58,896 --> 02:07:00,979
You can't stop me.

1721
02:07:02,104 --> 02:07:03,979
No! You can't stop me.

1722
02:07:04,396 --> 02:07:05,646
My mother is here.

1723
02:07:06,812 --> 02:07:10,396
My sister is here. And my life is here.

1724
02:07:12,396 --> 02:07:14,479
My whole life is here.

1725
02:07:15,521 --> 02:07:21,271
<i>See! I went nowhere, here I am</i>

1726
02:07:24,521 --> 02:07:26,937
<i>But no longer the same</i>

1727
02:07:29,062 --> 02:07:32,187
<i>No longer the same</i>

1728
02:07:33,437 --> 02:07:36,896
<i>No longer the same</i>

1729
02:07:43,437 --> 02:07:45,187
This is a special travel permit.

1730
02:07:45,396 --> 02:07:47,646
It was issued a few years after Partition

1731
02:07:47,771 --> 02:07:50,104
for travel between India and Pakistan.

1732
02:07:50,187 --> 02:07:52,521
It was used once, in 1953.

1733
02:07:54,271 --> 02:07:57,396
So father went back? To Sargodha?

1734
02:07:58,062 --> 02:07:59,354
He must have.

1735
02:08:01,979 --> 02:08:03,979
So one of them could be the father?

1736
02:08:05,021 --> 02:08:06,187
And the other guy?

1737
02:08:09,937 --> 02:08:11,229
Father, look at this.

1738
02:08:11,771 --> 02:08:14,062
Is this you, in Sargodha?

1739
02:08:14,771 --> 02:08:16,979
Did you go back there with uncle
in 1953?

1740
02:08:18,646 --> 02:08:21,562
Six years after Partition.
Remember?

1741
02:08:23,687 --> 02:08:25,396
<i>I'm so happy you're here!</i>

1742
02:08:25,562 --> 02:08:27,687
Uncle, maybe you both went back

1743
02:08:28,271 --> 02:08:30,896
to find out what happened
to the women.

1744
02:08:31,396 --> 02:08:32,646
What happened there?

1745
02:08:34,437 --> 02:08:40,437
My brother said, "Pali, this world
has ended."

1746
02:08:40,937 --> 02:08:42,687
"Don't look back."

1747
02:08:42,937 --> 02:08:44,812
But he's looking back now.

1748
02:08:46,687 --> 02:08:49,104
He talks about Sargodha,
day and night.

1749
02:08:50,896 --> 02:08:52,479
Why must you be so stubborn?

1750
02:08:53,562 --> 02:08:56,687
Why do you refuse to talk
about what happened at Partition?

1751
02:08:58,479 --> 02:08:59,646
Of course I could talk!

1752
02:09:00,687 --> 02:09:02,271
But you wouldn't understand.

1753
02:09:03,687 --> 02:09:05,771
You'd think it was about hatred.

1754
02:09:06,771 --> 02:09:10,937
Hatred between two communities. Enmity.

1755
02:09:11,687 --> 02:09:14,562
But that time was something else.

1756
02:09:15,312 --> 02:09:19,646
Human behaviour? The poison
that was showered on us

1757
02:09:20,146 --> 02:09:21,771
that was something else.

1758
02:09:21,979 --> 02:09:25,562
But it shall burn to ashes with us.
We shall not share it.

1759
02:09:27,271 --> 02:09:28,687
Will it really end with you?

1760
02:09:30,687 --> 02:09:31,854
Are you sure?

1761
02:09:33,604 --> 02:09:36,729
Maybe after you go, that poison
will enter us too.

1762
02:09:36,812 --> 02:09:37,937
Nivi...

1763
02:09:39,854 --> 02:09:41,812
One thing is certain

1764
02:09:41,979 --> 02:09:44,354
whatever grandpa is looking for

1765
02:09:44,521 --> 02:09:47,271
if he doesn't find it,
he'll leave that unrest to us.

1766
02:09:48,521 --> 02:09:50,271
The question will torment us.

1767
02:09:50,521 --> 02:09:52,604
What was troubling him so much?

1768
02:09:54,771 --> 02:09:56,396
Perhaps we could've helped him.

1769
02:09:57,396 --> 02:09:58,396
But we didn't.

1770
02:10:00,521 --> 02:10:02,979
After living a long and traumatic life

1771
02:10:04,146 --> 02:10:05,979
he couldn't even die in peace.

1772
02:10:21,104 --> 02:10:23,521
So many years have passed.

1773
02:10:27,062 --> 02:10:28,562
I witnessed it all.

1774
02:10:30,646 --> 02:10:32,729
But those memories...

1775
02:10:34,271 --> 02:10:37,021
I still wonder whether any of it
really happened.

1776
02:10:39,146 --> 02:10:40,521
Or was it my imagination?

1777
02:10:43,437 --> 02:10:44,479
I don't know.

1778
02:10:59,771 --> 02:11:01,021
Why are we here, Brother?

1779
02:11:03,604 --> 02:11:05,687
We'll come back here later.

1780
02:11:07,021 --> 02:11:07,937
Come.

1781
02:11:13,187 --> 02:11:14,521
Why are you here?

1782
02:11:17,979 --> 02:11:19,396
You shouldn't have come.

1783
02:11:23,562 --> 02:11:24,771
Nothing's left.

1784
02:11:54,187 --> 02:11:55,229
Afzal?

1785
02:11:55,687 --> 02:11:56,812
May I enter?

1786
02:11:58,729 --> 02:12:01,229
No religion teaches us
to harm others.

1787
02:12:02,521 --> 02:12:03,687
Traitor!

1788
02:12:04,062 --> 02:12:06,271
Afzal, stop!

1789
02:12:06,479 --> 02:12:08,562
Stop! Stop!

1790
02:12:10,187 --> 02:12:11,437
Aftab!

1791
02:12:18,604 --> 02:12:22,937
Brother's close friend,
Aftab.

1792
02:12:37,687 --> 02:12:39,062
What did you find out?

1793
02:12:41,771 --> 02:12:42,687
Grandpa Pali?

1794
02:13:12,396 --> 02:13:13,479
Send her to me.

1795
02:13:22,271 --> 02:13:23,604
And her!

1796
02:13:33,187 --> 02:13:35,271
And her!

1797
02:13:36,104 --> 02:13:37,729
Sit down!

1798
02:13:38,604 --> 02:13:40,271
O Lord!

1799
02:13:40,854 --> 02:13:42,937
Send her!

1800
02:13:46,104 --> 02:13:47,312
Next!

1801
02:13:47,729 --> 02:13:52,729
Sir, you did not do the right thing.

1802
02:13:55,771 --> 02:13:57,479
Next!

1803
02:14:00,604 --> 02:14:02,229
Dear Lord!

1804
02:14:04,437 --> 02:14:05,604
Come.

1805
02:14:06,771 --> 02:14:09,437
Sir, you did not do the right thing.

1806
02:14:09,646 --> 02:14:10,687
Afzal!

1807
02:14:21,729 --> 02:14:22,771
Tell Jasmer...

1808
02:14:25,646 --> 02:14:27,187
you didn't find Muzaffar.

1809
02:14:28,479 --> 02:14:29,562
He died.

1810
02:14:31,479 --> 02:14:32,687
And tell him...

1811
02:14:35,979 --> 02:14:37,396
they died too.

1812
02:14:40,479 --> 02:14:42,396
What have we done to deserve this?

1813
02:14:43,687 --> 02:14:45,771
Nothing can be done now.

1814
02:14:56,271 --> 02:14:59,896
You mean grandpa found out
what happened to the women.

1815
02:15:01,479 --> 02:15:02,896
Your grandpa knows.

1816
02:15:03,479 --> 02:15:04,979
They were finished off.

1817
02:15:06,896 --> 02:15:09,354
Then what remains unfinished in his mind?

1818
02:15:11,479 --> 02:15:13,562
What keeps obsessing him?

1819
02:15:20,896 --> 02:15:22,771
My wife wants to meet you.

1820
02:15:24,062 --> 02:15:25,396
She knows you.

1821
02:15:26,271 --> 02:15:27,896
She was your classmate.

1822
02:15:29,104 --> 02:15:31,187
Yours and Aftab's.

1823
02:15:33,354 --> 02:15:34,396
Afsana.

1824
02:15:35,396 --> 02:15:36,312
Remember?

1825
02:15:39,562 --> 02:15:42,229
Terrible things happened to her
after Partition.

1826
02:15:42,771 --> 02:15:45,146
They framed her father as a traitor.

1827
02:15:45,604 --> 02:15:47,646
They set fire to his printing press.

1828
02:15:48,062 --> 02:15:50,187
Then one night he was killed.

1829
02:15:50,771 --> 02:15:53,729
Afsana was all alone
and in grave danger.

1830
02:15:54,187 --> 02:15:56,271
So father got us married.

1831
02:15:56,979 --> 02:15:59,062
She always said that you'd come back.

1832
02:16:00,062 --> 02:16:03,729
I told her you were here.
She's waiting.

1833
02:16:05,062 --> 02:16:07,229
Wait here, I'll call her.

1834
02:16:21,562 --> 02:16:22,562
Pali.

1835
02:16:24,646 --> 02:16:26,271
This world has ended.

1836
02:16:29,687 --> 02:16:31,104
Don't look back.

1837
02:16:35,146 --> 02:16:36,104
Let's go.

1838
02:17:05,021 --> 02:17:09,812
<i>Just like you are near me</i>

1839
02:17:10,229 --> 02:17:14,271
<i>I am near you</i>

1840
02:17:15,062 --> 02:17:19,896
<i>Like evenings melt into the dawn</i>

1841
02:17:20,187 --> 02:17:24,062
<i>I am near you</i>

1842
02:17:24,354 --> 02:17:30,146
<i>Like a poem written on a blank page</i>

1843
02:17:30,312 --> 02:17:34,479
<i>Or a forgotten promise</i>

1844
02:17:35,396 --> 02:17:40,187
<i>Just like the thirsty stay near water</i>

1845
02:17:40,521 --> 02:17:45,146
<i>I am near you</i>

1846
02:17:45,479 --> 02:17:50,437
<i>Like a stirring of the heart</i>

1847
02:17:50,521 --> 02:17:52,812
No one would dare harm you.

1848
02:17:53,896 --> 02:17:56,312
Sounds better in a poet's voice.

1849
02:18:05,187 --> 02:18:07,562
- Jaswant!
- Yes, Father!

1850
02:18:07,646 --> 02:18:09,062
The curse will get me too.

1851
02:18:09,146 --> 02:18:11,062
I'll die from shock someday.

1852
02:18:11,146 --> 02:18:12,937
- Jaswant!
- Yes.

1853
02:18:13,021 --> 02:18:14,937
Tell the driver to bring
the car round.

1854
02:18:15,021 --> 02:18:16,896
Sir, the driver's gone home.

1855
02:18:16,979 --> 02:18:18,896
Take me for an idiot?

1856
02:18:19,479 --> 02:18:22,437
He lives here. Go and call him.

1857
02:18:22,562 --> 02:18:25,104
- Stay away. Let me...
- Sir, please. I can't...

1858
02:18:25,187 --> 02:18:27,562
- Sir, relax. You can't do that!
- Grandpa, relax.

1859
02:18:27,646 --> 02:18:28,562
I...

1860
02:18:28,646 --> 02:18:29,854
I am...

1861
02:18:29,937 --> 02:18:30,771
Get away!

1862
02:18:30,854 --> 02:18:32,854
I'm ready. Let's go.

1863
02:18:32,937 --> 02:18:36,396
You were writing a poem.
Let's finish it first.

1864
02:18:36,479 --> 02:18:37,521
I...

1865
02:18:37,646 --> 02:18:39,646
It's finished? Very good.

1866
02:18:39,771 --> 02:18:41,396
- I don't have time.
- Oh, God!

1867
02:18:41,479 --> 02:18:44,354
- I'm running out of time. Let's go.
- Grandpa, please rest.

1868
02:18:44,437 --> 02:18:45,771
Lie back, please.

1869
02:18:46,146 --> 02:18:47,354
- Lie back.
- You...

1870
02:18:47,896 --> 02:18:49,979
You promised me.

1871
02:18:51,062 --> 02:18:53,771
I trusted you.

1872
02:18:53,854 --> 02:18:57,312
I'll take you. But the poetry
recital hasn't started.

1873
02:18:57,437 --> 02:18:59,771
Oxygen cylinder! Quick!

1874
02:18:59,854 --> 02:19:02,354
Grandpa, you want to read your poem
at the recital?

1875
02:19:02,771 --> 02:19:03,896
I'll take you.

1876
02:19:03,979 --> 02:19:05,729
The poetry recital hasn't started.

1877
02:19:05,812 --> 02:19:08,771
Not the recital. Jiya.

1878
02:19:09,062 --> 02:19:10,062
Move aside, please.

1879
02:19:10,396 --> 02:19:12,562
Jiya.

1880
02:19:14,771 --> 02:19:15,771
Jiya?

1881
02:19:22,771 --> 02:19:24,271
Queen Dilfareb?

1882
02:19:30,687 --> 02:19:31,896
<i>Is it possible</i>

1883
02:19:33,604 --> 02:19:35,687
<i>to still love a girl</i>

1884
02:19:36,771 --> 02:19:39,479
<i>that one has not seen for 78 years?</i>

1885
02:19:41,812 --> 02:19:45,312
<i>He's forgotten his family,
can't remember his home.</i>

1886
02:19:46,312 --> 02:19:47,896
<i>But he remembers her.</i>

1887
02:19:50,479 --> 02:19:52,562
All he remembers is her.

1888
02:19:54,979 --> 02:19:55,979
Jiya.

1889
02:19:57,771 --> 02:20:01,187
He wants to see Jiya
to recite his poem to her.

1890
02:20:03,354 --> 02:20:05,937
He can't die without seeing her.

1891
02:20:08,021 --> 02:20:10,896
Could anyone feel like that about me?

1892
02:20:13,562 --> 02:20:15,979
Could I ever feel like that about anyone?

1893
02:20:17,187 --> 02:20:18,687
<i>What is it, Nirvair?</i>

1894
02:20:19,562 --> 02:20:21,187
Those were different times.

1895
02:20:22,354 --> 02:20:25,396
A love like this was only possible
in the old days.

1896
02:20:26,146 --> 02:20:28,396
<i>We can't get that kind of love anymore.</i>

1897
02:20:28,646 --> 02:20:31,187
We don't get this kind of love.

1898
02:20:33,479 --> 02:20:34,687
I know that much.

1899
02:20:36,271 --> 02:20:38,062
It exists within us.

1900
02:20:39,146 --> 02:20:42,271
You know, this love is like a poison.

1901
02:20:44,354 --> 02:20:46,146
It must flow out.

1902
02:20:48,104 --> 02:20:49,979
All our lives we look for something

1903
02:20:51,187 --> 02:20:53,021
to transfer it to

1904
02:20:54,562 --> 02:20:55,854
to someone

1905
02:20:57,146 --> 02:20:58,687
to work, to a passion.

1906
02:21:00,104 --> 02:21:02,229
Something that can hold this love.

1907
02:21:04,562 --> 02:21:05,771
It's so dangerous...

1908
02:21:08,062 --> 02:21:10,354
that if a single drop of it
stays within us...

1909
02:21:13,062 --> 02:21:15,646
it won't let us die in peace.

1910
02:21:17,687 --> 02:21:19,896
<i>I want that kind of love.</i>

1911
02:21:24,854 --> 02:21:25,854
I want it too.

1912
02:21:41,771 --> 02:21:44,312
I'm calling the ambulance.
It's an emergency.

1913
02:21:44,562 --> 02:21:45,521
No.

1914
02:21:46,104 --> 02:21:47,396
What do you mean "no"?

1915
02:21:47,979 --> 02:21:49,312
He's had another stroke.

1916
02:21:51,104 --> 02:21:52,687
He's not leaving home again.

1917
02:21:54,104 --> 02:21:55,771
He'll stay at home.

1918
02:21:57,312 --> 02:21:58,771
If he dies, he dies here.

1919
02:22:01,479 --> 02:22:02,687
Help him here.

1920
02:22:14,521 --> 02:22:16,521
<i>When does the immigration office close?</i>

1921
02:22:16,771 --> 02:22:19,771
Yes, I have a British passport.
I have a visa.

1922
02:22:20,771 --> 02:22:22,187
Four hours maximum.

1923
02:22:22,354 --> 02:22:24,687
Well, let me see.

1924
02:22:24,771 --> 02:22:26,479
Father gave you an address, right?

1925
02:22:26,604 --> 02:22:28,187
- Yes, he did.
- Where is it?

1926
02:22:28,354 --> 02:22:30,187
Here! This is it.

1927
02:22:35,187 --> 02:22:36,271
Thank you, sir.

1928
02:22:58,771 --> 02:23:00,687
Nivi has crossed the border.

1929
02:23:02,479 --> 02:23:04,396
Four hours, ten minutes to Sargodha.

1930
02:23:04,771 --> 02:23:05,979
Get in front.

1931
02:23:06,062 --> 02:23:07,562
He's driving there now.

1932
02:23:08,062 --> 02:23:09,146
Are you listening?

1933
02:23:19,979 --> 02:23:22,146
Grandpa filled many notebooks.

1934
02:23:22,896 --> 02:23:26,521
No one in my family can read Urdu anymore,
so we don't know what it says.

1935
02:23:28,937 --> 02:23:30,521
<i>The madness of time</i>

1936
02:23:31,604 --> 02:23:33,229
<i>Something did happen</i>

1937
02:23:33,896 --> 02:23:35,187
He's a terrible poet!

1938
02:23:36,062 --> 02:23:37,479
I was warned.

1939
02:23:37,854 --> 02:23:39,562
But he has a big heart.

1940
02:23:42,187 --> 02:23:44,062
Some say he has no heart.

1941
02:23:44,687 --> 02:23:46,771
He left it here.

1942
02:23:48,479 --> 02:23:50,062
<i>If I go away</i>

1943
02:23:50,771 --> 02:23:53,771
<i>Then I would no longer be me</i>

1944
02:23:54,896 --> 02:23:57,146
<i>How could I leave myself behind?</i>

1945
02:23:57,896 --> 02:24:00,104
<i>Alone on your path</i>

1946
02:24:00,896 --> 02:24:03,437
<i>How could I reveal myself</i>

1947
02:24:04,187 --> 02:24:06,062
<i>In a gathering of strangers?</i>

1948
02:24:06,604 --> 02:24:07,687
How unfair!

1949
02:24:09,187 --> 02:24:12,187
He must've felt like an outsider
all his life.

1950
02:24:13,604 --> 02:24:17,229
Like living in a stranger's property.

1951
02:24:20,687 --> 02:24:23,562
No matter how successful or how rich

1952
02:24:25,187 --> 02:24:27,854
one never has the confidence
of owning the place.

1953
02:24:32,062 --> 02:24:35,187
A strange feeling of guilt
lingers on

1954
02:24:36,479 --> 02:24:38,187
that I am somehow wrong

1955
02:24:39,687 --> 02:24:41,896
despite doing everything right.

1956
02:24:43,271 --> 02:24:45,854
This thought keeps haunting me...

1957
02:24:47,604 --> 02:24:49,687
Why am I here?

1958
02:24:51,396 --> 02:24:52,604
What am I doing?

1959
02:24:56,354 --> 02:24:58,646
I suddenly realized I was talking
about myself

1960
02:24:58,979 --> 02:25:01,271
and not grandpa.

1961
02:25:01,479 --> 02:25:04,479
Did I come here to solve his problem?
Or is he solving mine?

1962
02:25:04,854 --> 02:25:05,812
What do you mean?

1963
02:25:06,979 --> 02:25:08,687
He had no choice, Dad.

1964
02:25:09,187 --> 02:25:11,896
His home was taken away from him,
he was thrown out.

1965
02:25:13,271 --> 02:25:15,687
But why am I depriving myself of home?

1966
02:25:17,146 --> 02:25:18,812
Why have I exiled myself?

1967
02:25:19,479 --> 02:25:21,146
What are you trying to say?

1968
02:25:21,771 --> 02:25:24,729
You asked why I would not settle down.

1969
02:25:26,604 --> 02:25:28,479
I can never settle in the West,
Dad.

1970
02:25:29,646 --> 02:25:31,062
I'll keep wandering about.

1971
02:25:32,271 --> 02:25:33,979
Grandpa could never return

1972
02:25:34,979 --> 02:25:37,604
but I'll come back to India.

1973
02:25:38,354 --> 02:25:39,854
What will you do here?

1974
02:25:40,479 --> 02:25:41,979
There's so much to do, Dad.

1975
02:25:42,479 --> 02:25:45,062
There are real opportunities there,
and need too.

1976
02:25:46,396 --> 02:25:47,479
I have a feeling

1977
02:25:48,896 --> 02:25:50,687
grandpa is bringing me back home.

1978
02:25:53,062 --> 02:25:55,271
So come back home.

1979
02:26:18,479 --> 02:26:20,312
Do you know the Yellow Mansion?

1980
02:26:20,396 --> 02:26:22,104
- Royal Kulfi?
- No.

1981
02:26:32,271 --> 02:26:35,479
What's wrong? Doctor, what's happening?

1982
02:26:40,854 --> 02:26:42,062
<i>What happened?</i>

1983
02:26:42,396 --> 02:26:44,979
Maybe he had a stroke again
while asleep last night.

1984
02:26:45,062 --> 02:26:46,854
They're putting him on a ventilator.

1985
02:26:46,937 --> 02:26:48,437
He's a fighter. No ventilator.

1986
02:26:48,854 --> 02:26:50,937
How much more can he fight? He's done.

1987
02:26:51,021 --> 02:26:53,937
I'm in Sargodha. Tell him.

1988
02:26:54,271 --> 02:26:57,771
Dear, it's too late.

1989
02:26:59,604 --> 02:27:01,187
He isn't even opening his eyes.

1990
02:27:01,271 --> 02:27:04,521
He'll have to open his eyes.
Tell him I am in Sargodha.

1991
02:27:04,812 --> 02:27:07,479
- Nirvair, please... let it go.
- Father.

1992
02:27:08,104 --> 02:27:11,271
Father! Nivi is in Sargodha.
Sargodha, Father.

1993
02:27:11,354 --> 02:27:14,312
Show it to him, it's Sargodha!

1994
02:27:14,521 --> 02:27:16,021
Grandpa, look it's Sargodha.

1995
02:27:17,229 --> 02:27:19,729
Look, Father! Sargodha.

1996
02:27:20,521 --> 02:27:21,812
<i>Grandpa, look!</i>

1997
02:27:22,687 --> 02:27:26,396
I told you I'd bring you here.
Look!

1998
02:27:27,354 --> 02:27:29,271
- He's opening his eyes, Nivi.
- Yes.

1999
02:27:29,354 --> 02:27:31,687
Yes! Look, this is your Sargodha.

2000
02:27:32,312 --> 02:27:34,979
Look, Father. Nivi is there.

2001
02:27:35,104 --> 02:27:36,896
Look, Grandpa. Recognize it?

2002
02:27:37,604 --> 02:27:41,229
There's an old building over there,
from his time.

2003
02:27:41,812 --> 02:27:44,062
- Where is it?
- Behind that tree.

2004
02:27:44,646 --> 02:27:45,812
That's it.

2005
02:27:50,104 --> 02:27:52,812
Grandpa, recognize this building?
It's in Sargodha.

2006
02:27:53,146 --> 02:27:56,271
<i>- Look carefully.</i>
- Recognize it?

2007
02:27:57,896 --> 02:27:58,812
Yes, Nivi.

2008
02:28:01,312 --> 02:28:02,479
Turn right.

2009
02:28:02,854 --> 02:28:03,854
What?

2010
02:28:04,271 --> 02:28:06,604
Turn right!

2011
02:28:07,604 --> 02:28:08,604
Yes!

2012
02:28:11,354 --> 02:28:13,312
Go straight.

2013
02:28:13,521 --> 02:28:14,687
Yes, Grandpa, straight.

2014
02:28:15,896 --> 02:28:16,771
Straight...

2015
02:28:17,229 --> 02:28:19,312
Walking straight.

2016
02:28:21,146 --> 02:28:22,396
Grandpa...

2017
02:28:24,937 --> 02:28:27,354
<i>Grandpa, left or right?</i>

2018
02:28:27,854 --> 02:28:31,854
- Straight.
- He's getting confused.

2019
02:28:31,979 --> 02:28:34,146
The streets must have changed
in 78 years.

2020
02:28:34,354 --> 02:28:35,271
One second.

2021
02:28:40,771 --> 02:28:42,854
Yes, straight.

2022
02:28:45,146 --> 02:28:48,354
The church... on the right...

2023
02:28:50,062 --> 02:28:52,146
Over there!

2024
02:28:55,437 --> 02:28:56,937
We're at the church!

2025
02:28:58,646 --> 02:29:00,812
Turn left.

2026
02:29:01,354 --> 02:29:03,437
I'm turning left.

2027
02:29:15,104 --> 02:29:16,229
Yes, Grandpa.

2028
02:29:17,687 --> 02:29:19,771
Turn right, turn right.

2029
02:29:50,271 --> 02:29:51,896
Is this it, Father?

2030
02:29:52,687 --> 02:29:55,437
Dad, I'll call back in two minutes.
Don't let him sleep.

2031
02:29:55,521 --> 02:29:56,646
Nivi, don't hang up.

2032
02:29:56,771 --> 02:29:58,562
There are new buildings everywhere.

2033
02:29:58,646 --> 02:30:00,896
<i>He'll get confused.
We'll have to ask someone.</i>

2034
02:30:00,979 --> 02:30:02,646
Let's take a break, Father.

2035
02:30:04,479 --> 02:30:06,562
He'll call back in a minute.

2036
02:30:07,271 --> 02:30:08,354
Greetings.

2037
02:30:08,979 --> 02:30:11,729
They say a newspaper man's family
lived here.

2038
02:30:13,187 --> 02:30:15,396
Their printing press was set on fire.

2039
02:30:15,562 --> 02:30:19,146
Printing press? He mentioned it
in his diary.

2040
02:30:20,771 --> 02:30:23,187
Where do they live now?

2041
02:30:24,104 --> 02:30:25,062
I don't know.

2042
02:30:26,187 --> 02:30:29,396
But ask at the house
of the Ahmedzai family.

2043
02:30:29,854 --> 02:30:31,187
Ahmedzai?

2044
02:30:31,271 --> 02:30:34,354
The newspaper man's daughter
married an Ahmedzai.

2045
02:30:41,521 --> 02:30:42,687
Jiya!

2046
02:30:42,771 --> 02:30:43,687
- Hey.
- Jiya!

2047
02:30:43,771 --> 02:30:44,812
What are we doing?

2048
02:30:45,812 --> 02:30:46,771
This way.

2049
02:30:49,354 --> 02:30:50,312
Come.

2050
02:30:53,146 --> 02:30:56,229
You said I would forget you
when the time comes.

2051
02:30:56,354 --> 02:30:57,771
Your name would rub off.

2052
02:30:59,687 --> 02:31:00,604
Look at this.

2053
02:31:01,687 --> 02:31:03,271
Indelible ink.

2054
02:31:04,771 --> 02:31:06,104
It'll never fade!

2055
02:31:07,354 --> 02:31:08,687
I'll never forget you.

2056
02:31:10,104 --> 02:31:11,854
You always say such foolish things.

2057
02:31:12,979 --> 02:31:15,062
- That's just me.
- Yes.

2058
02:31:15,604 --> 02:31:18,396
I won't go, no matter what happens.

2059
02:31:19,146 --> 02:31:22,187
I keep praying nothing bad happens

2060
02:31:22,271 --> 02:31:24,354
and you don't have to go.

2061
02:31:25,271 --> 02:31:27,354
But I don't know...

2062
02:31:27,729 --> 02:31:29,187
